1
00:00:17,872 --> 00:00:20,250
[Cheerful instrumental music]

2
00:02:23,706 --> 00:02:25,583
[Clock dinging]

3
00:02:54,779 --> 00:02:56,239
[Children screaming]

4
00:02:56,322 --> 00:02:58,283
All right, what's it going to be?

5
00:02:58,366 --> 00:03:00,785
A Triple Cream Cup for Christopher.

6
00:03:00,994 --> 00:03:03,454
A Squelchy Snorter for Otis.

7
00:03:03,538 --> 00:03:06,708
A Sizzler for June Marie. And listen.

8
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:09,460
BILL: Wonka's got a new one today.
CHILDREN: What is it?

9
00:03:09,544 --> 00:03:12,255
This is called a Scrumdiddleumptious Bar.

10
00:03:12,338 --> 00:03:15,091
Scrumdiddleumptious Bar?
How does he do it?

11
00:03:15,174 --> 00:03:18,011
- My boy, do you ask a fish how it swims?
- No.

12
00:03:18,094 --> 00:03:19,679
BILL: Or a bird how it flies?

13
00:03:19,762 --> 00:03:22,932
No sirree, you don't.
They do it because they were born to do it.

14
00:03:23,016 --> 00:03:26,227
Just like Willy Wonka
was born to be a candy man...

15
00:03:26,311 --> 00:03:29,189
and you look like
you were born to be a Wonkerer.

16
00:03:29,272 --> 00:03:32,358
<i>[Singing] Who can take the sunrise</i>

17
00:03:32,692 --> 00:03:35,361
<i>Sprinkle it with dew</i>

18
00:03:36,237 --> 00:03:39,824
<i>Cover it in chocolate and a miracle or two?</i>

19
00:03:39,991 --> 00:03:41,826
The Candy Man.

20
00:03:43,077 --> 00:03:45,038
<i>[Singing] The Candy Man can</i>

21
00:03:46,581 --> 00:03:49,459
<i>The Candy Man can
'Cause he mixes it with love</i>

22
00:03:49,542 --> 00:03:51,920
<i>And makes the world taste good</i>

23
00:03:53,713 --> 00:03:56,883
<i>Who can take a rainbow</i>

24
00:03:57,091 --> 00:03:59,052
<i>Wrap it in a sigh</i>

25
00:04:00,553 --> 00:04:04,182
<i>Soak it in the sun
And make the strawberry-lemon pie?</i>

26
00:04:04,390 --> 00:04:07,060
CHILDREN: The Candy Man?
BILL: The Candy Man.

27
00:04:08,436 --> 00:04:10,271
The Candy Man can.

28
00:04:11,105 --> 00:04:14,234
<i>[Singing] The Candy Man can
'Cause he mixes it with love</i>

29
00:04:14,317 --> 00:04:16,903
<i>And makes the world taste good</i>

30
00:04:18,238 --> 00:04:19,822
<i>Willy Wonka makes</i>

31
00:04:19,906 --> 00:04:21,407
<i>Everything he bakes</i>

32
00:04:21,616 --> 00:04:24,953
<i>Satisfying and delicious</i>

33
00:04:25,036 --> 00:04:27,872
<i>Talk about your childhood wishes</i>

34
00:04:28,289 --> 00:04:31,626
<i>You can even eat the dishes</i>

35
00:04:35,213 --> 00:04:37,340
[Lively instrumental music]

36
00:04:42,178 --> 00:04:44,472
<i>[Singing] Who can take tomorrow</i>

37
00:04:44,556 --> 00:04:46,724
<i>Dip it in a dream</i>

38
00:04:47,433 --> 00:04:50,854
<i>Separate the sorrow
and collect up all the cream?</i>

39
00:04:50,937 --> 00:04:53,940
<i>BILL: The Candy Man
CHILDREN: Willy Wonka can</i>

40
00:04:54,148 --> 00:04:56,818
<i>The Candy Man can</i>

41
00:04:58,111 --> 00:05:02,240
<i>The Candy Man can
'Cause he mixes it with love</i>

42
00:05:02,323 --> 00:05:06,035
<i>And makes the world taste good</i>

43
00:05:07,829 --> 00:05:09,831
<i>And the world tastes good</i>

44
00:05:10,999 --> 00:05:16,129
<i>'Cause the Candy Man thinks it should</i>

45
00:05:35,690 --> 00:05:37,108
CHARLIE: Hi, Mr. Jopeck.

46
00:05:37,192 --> 00:05:39,861
MR. JOPECK: Come along, Charlie.
You're late.

47
00:05:41,821 --> 00:05:44,490
CHARLIE: It's payday, Mr. Jopeck.
MR. JOPECK: You're right.

48
00:05:45,533 --> 00:05:47,869
MR. JOPECK: There you are.
CHARLIE: Thanks.

49
00:05:48,661 --> 00:05:49,746
Say hello to your Grandpa Joe.

50
00:05:49,829 --> 00:05:50,955
CHARLIE: Okay.

51
00:05:53,166 --> 00:05:55,585
[Fast-paced instrumental music]

52
00:06:06,429 --> 00:06:08,640
[Eerie instrumental music]

53
00:06:33,915 --> 00:06:36,042
THE TINKER: " Up the airy mountain

54
00:06:36,251 --> 00:06:38,503
" Down the rushing glen

55
00:06:38,711 --> 00:06:40,880
" We dare not go a-hunting

56
00:06:41,047 --> 00:06:43,967
" For fear of little men"

57
00:06:44,384 --> 00:06:48,721
You see, nobody ever goes in...

58
00:06:52,183 --> 00:06:54,227
and nobody ever comes out.

59
00:07:04,654 --> 00:07:06,114
Charlie's late.

60
00:07:06,573 --> 00:07:09,242
He works too hard for a little boy.

61
00:07:09,450 --> 00:07:11,452
<i>GRANDPA: He should have
some time to play.</i>

62
00:07:11,536 --> 00:07:13,204
Not enough hours in the day.

63
00:07:13,288 --> 00:07:16,416
MRS. BUCKET: With the four of you
bedridden for the past 20 years...

64
00:07:16,499 --> 00:07:19,002
it takes a lot of work
to keep this family going.

65
00:07:19,085 --> 00:07:21,379
If only his father were alive.

66
00:07:21,963 --> 00:07:25,717
As soon as I get my strength back,
I'll get out of this bed and help him.

67
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:29,262
<i>In all the years you've been saying
you'll get out of that bed...</i>

68
00:07:29,345 --> 00:07:32,181
<i>I've yet to see you set foot on the floor.</i>

69
00:07:33,266 --> 00:07:35,935
Maybe if the floor wasn't so cold...

70
00:07:37,270 --> 00:07:38,730
Hi, everybody.

71
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:42,108
Wake up, Charlie's home.

72
00:07:43,443 --> 00:07:44,986
CHARLIE: Grandpa George.

73
00:07:46,446 --> 00:07:48,114
CHARLIE: Grandma Georgina.

74
00:07:48,448 --> 00:07:50,200
CHARLIE: Grandma Josephine.

75
00:07:51,326 --> 00:07:53,161
CHARLIE: Grandpa Joe.

76
00:07:55,622 --> 00:07:57,874
Is this your supper, Grandpa?

77
00:07:57,957 --> 00:07:59,709
It's yours, too, Charlie.

78
00:07:59,792 --> 00:08:02,754
I'm fed up with cabbage water.
It's not enough.

79
00:08:02,837 --> 00:08:05,423
JOSEPHINE: It's all we have.
GRANDPA: What are you saying?

80
00:08:06,674 --> 00:08:09,552
CHARLIE: How about this?
MRS. BUCKET: Where did you get that?

81
00:08:09,636 --> 00:08:13,223
What difference does it make
where he got it? Point is, he got it.

82
00:08:13,306 --> 00:08:17,477
<i>- It's my first payday.
- Good for you. We'll have a real banquet.</i>

83
00:08:17,685 --> 00:08:21,689
CHARLIE: Mom, here's what's left.
You keep it.

84
00:08:22,857 --> 00:08:24,484
Except for this.

85
00:08:24,692 --> 00:08:27,779
From now on,
I'm going to pay for your tobacco.

86
00:08:28,696 --> 00:08:31,991
No one's going to pay for it.
I'm giving it up.

87
00:08:32,325 --> 00:08:34,744
Come on, Dad, it's only one pipe a day.

88
00:08:34,827 --> 00:08:38,456
<i>When a loaf of bread looks like a banquet,
I've no right buying tobacco.</i>

89
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:41,709
Go on, Grandpa. Please take it.

90
00:08:49,217 --> 00:08:53,012
CHARLIE: After I finished my paper route,
I was in front of Wonka's.

91
00:08:53,513 --> 00:08:57,559
<i>There was this strange man there.
I think he was a tinker.</i>

92
00:08:58,059 --> 00:09:01,479
<i>He was standing right behind me
looking up at the factory.</i>

93
00:09:01,688 --> 00:09:03,815
<i>Just before he left, he said.:</i>

94
00:09:04,023 --> 00:09:07,318
" Nobody ever goes in,
and nobody ever comes out. "

95
00:09:07,527 --> 00:09:09,487
And right he was, Charlie.

96
00:09:09,571 --> 00:09:12,490
Not since the tragic day
that Willy Wonka locked it.

97
00:09:12,574 --> 00:09:13,825
CHARLIE: Why did he lock it?

98
00:09:13,908 --> 00:09:17,328
Because all the other chocolate makers
were sending in spies...

99
00:09:17,412 --> 00:09:20,874
dressed as workers
to steal Mr. Wonka's secret recipes.

100
00:09:21,082 --> 00:09:25,044
Especially Slugworth.
That Slugworth was the worst.

101
00:09:25,587 --> 00:09:29,841
Finally, Mr. Wonka shouted,
" I shall be ruined! Close the factory. "

102
00:09:29,966 --> 00:09:32,468
And that's just what he did.

103
00:09:32,552 --> 00:09:36,097
He locked the gates
and vanished completely.

104
00:09:36,389 --> 00:09:39,350
And then suddenly,
about three years later...

105
00:09:39,434 --> 00:09:42,020
the most amazing thing happened.

106
00:09:42,228 --> 00:09:44,981
The factory started working again,
full blast.

107
00:09:45,064 --> 00:09:48,526
And more delicious candies
were coming out than ever before.

108
00:09:48,610 --> 00:09:51,738
But the gates stayed locked,
so that no one...

109
00:09:51,946 --> 00:09:54,824
not even Mr. Slugworth, could steal them.

110
00:09:54,908 --> 00:09:59,037
But, Grandpa, someone must be helping
Mr. Wonka work the factory.

111
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:00,914
GRANDPA: Thousands
must be helping him.

112
00:10:00,997 --> 00:10:03,499
But who? Who are they?

113
00:10:04,459 --> 00:10:07,712
That is the biggest mystery of them all.

114
00:10:09,672 --> 00:10:11,758
TURKENTINE: Charlie Bucket?
CHARLIE: Yes, sir?

115
00:10:11,841 --> 00:10:15,053
<i>TURKENTINE: I shall need an assistant.
Come and give me a hand.</i>

116
00:10:17,180 --> 00:10:19,349
<i>We have here nitric acid, glycerin...</i>

117
00:10:19,432 --> 00:10:21,518
and a special mixture of my own.

118
00:10:21,601 --> 00:10:24,604
Together, it's horrible, dangerous stuff.
Blows you up.

119
00:10:24,687 --> 00:10:28,107
But mixed together right, as only
I know how, what do you think it makes?

120
00:10:28,191 --> 00:10:30,360
CHARLIE: I don't know.
TURKENTINE: Of course not...

121
00:10:30,443 --> 00:10:32,028
because only I know.

122
00:10:32,111 --> 00:10:35,782
If you knew, you'd be teaching me
instead of me teaching you.

123
00:10:35,865 --> 00:10:40,620
And for a student to teach his teacher
is presumptuous and rude. Is that clear?

124
00:10:40,703 --> 00:10:42,038
CHARLIE: Yes, sir.
TURKENTINE: Good.

125
00:10:42,121 --> 00:10:46,834
Mixed together in the right way,
these three highly dangerous ingredients...

126
00:10:46,918 --> 00:10:49,295
make the finest wart remover
in the world.

127
00:10:49,379 --> 00:10:52,131
TURKENTINE: The trick is to pour them in
in equal amounts.

128
00:10:52,215 --> 00:10:55,468
Charlie, you take the nitric acid
and the glycerin...

129
00:10:55,593 --> 00:10:58,763
and I'll take my own special mixture.
Are you ready?

130
00:10:58,846 --> 00:11:00,890
Good lad. Pour.

131
00:11:01,516 --> 00:11:02,892
[Students laughing and clapping]

132
00:11:02,976 --> 00:11:04,519
Did we do it wrong?

133
00:11:04,811 --> 00:11:07,939
TURKENTINE: No, certainly not.
This is for very big warts.

134
00:11:08,147 --> 00:11:10,567
What on earth's going on out there?

135
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:12,569
STUDENT 1: I hope there's still some left.

136
00:11:12,777 --> 00:11:15,238
Winkelmann, come here.
What's happening?

137
00:11:15,321 --> 00:11:16,781
Willy Wonka's opening his factory.

138
00:11:16,865 --> 00:11:19,033
- He's gonna let people in.
- Are you sure?

139
00:11:19,117 --> 00:11:21,578
It's on the radio.
And he's giving chocolates away.

140
00:11:21,661 --> 00:11:22,704
Class dismissed.

141
00:11:22,787 --> 00:11:24,539
<i>WINKELMANN: No, it's only for five people.</i>

142
00:11:24,622 --> 00:11:26,291
Class un-dismissed.

143
00:11:26,374 --> 00:11:29,919
<i>He's hidden five Golden Tickets.
Whoever finds them wins the prize.</i>

144
00:11:30,003 --> 00:11:32,130
<i>- Where's he hidden them?
- In five Wonka Bars.</i>

145
00:11:33,506 --> 00:11:35,800
<i>- You've gotta buy them to find them.
- Class re-dismissed!</i>

146
00:11:39,137 --> 00:11:41,306
STUDENT 2: I'm going to buy
the whole store.

147
00:11:46,561 --> 00:11:49,063
<i>NEWSCASTER: Now, details
on the sudden announcement...</i>

148
00:11:49,147 --> 00:11:51,774
that has captured the attention
of the entire world.

149
00:11:51,858 --> 00:11:54,736
Hidden among the countless billions
of Wonka Bars...

150
00:11:54,819 --> 00:11:56,738
<i>are five Gold Tickets.</i>

151
00:11:56,821 --> 00:12:01,201
<i>And to the five people who find them
will come the most fabulous prize:</i>

152
00:12:01,367 --> 00:12:03,786
A lifetime supply of chocolate.

153
00:12:03,870 --> 00:12:05,663
<i>As if this were not enough...</i>

154
00:12:05,747 --> 00:12:09,751
<i>each winner, before receiving his prize,
will be personally escorted...</i>

155
00:12:09,834 --> 00:12:11,461
They're all crazy.

156
00:12:11,544 --> 00:12:14,881
GRANDPA: The man's a genius.
He'll sell a million bars.

157
00:12:15,715 --> 00:12:18,676
<i>Grandpa, do you think I've got a chance
to find one?</i>

158
00:12:18,760 --> 00:12:21,596
One? I'm counting on you to find all five.

159
00:12:21,679 --> 00:12:23,056
One's enough for me.

160
00:12:23,139 --> 00:12:26,392
<i>NEWSCASTER: We have reports coming in
that the response is phenomenal.</i>

161
00:12:26,476 --> 00:12:29,521
<i>Wonka Bars are disappearing
from candy store shelves...</i>

162
00:12:29,604 --> 00:12:31,606
<i>at a rate to boggle the mind.</i>

163
00:12:31,689 --> 00:12:33,274
<i>Truly, it is incredible...</i>

164
00:12:33,358 --> 00:12:36,277
<i>the way that Wonkamania
has descended upon the globe.</i>

165
00:12:36,611 --> 00:12:39,489
<i>While the world searches,
we watch and wait...</i>

166
00:12:39,572 --> 00:12:41,699
<i>wondering where the pursuit will lead...</i>

167
00:12:41,783 --> 00:12:45,119
<i>and how long the spirit of man will hold up
under the strain.</i>

168
00:12:45,203 --> 00:12:49,457
I'm still having these dreams, Doctor,
and I still can't stop believing them.

169
00:12:49,541 --> 00:12:51,501
<i>I've told you, Mr. Hoffstedder...</i>

170
00:12:51,584 --> 00:12:54,879
<i>to believe in one's dreams
is a manifestation of insanity.</i>

171
00:12:54,963 --> 00:12:58,091
The sooner you accept this,
the sooner you'll get well.

172
00:12:58,174 --> 00:13:01,302
But I dreamed the archangel appeared
and whispered into my ear...

173
00:13:01,386 --> 00:13:03,638
and told me where to find
a Golden Wonka Ticket.

174
00:13:03,721 --> 00:13:05,306
What exactly did he say?

175
00:13:05,390 --> 00:13:08,768
What difference does that make?
This was a dream, a fantasy.

176
00:13:08,852 --> 00:13:11,771
HOFFSTEDDER: You said-
DOCTOR: Just tell me where the ticket is!

177
00:13:12,188 --> 00:13:14,148
<i>We began with five Golden Tickets.</i>

178
00:13:14,232 --> 00:13:18,987
<i>Like five lucky bolts of lightning
ready to strike at any point on the map.</i>

179
00:13:19,070 --> 00:13:22,031
No one knew where or when
the first one would hit.

180
00:13:22,115 --> 00:13:24,993
But as you all know,
last night we got our answer.

181
00:13:25,076 --> 00:13:28,413
STANLEY: While we in America slept,
the first Golden Ticket was found...

182
00:13:28,496 --> 00:13:31,499
in the small town of Duselheim, Germany.

183
00:13:31,583 --> 00:13:34,502
<i>We've been waiting several hours
for the follow-up story.</i>

184
00:13:34,586 --> 00:13:36,296
<i>We're finally ready with a live report.</i>

185
00:13:36,963 --> 00:13:39,924
Proud we are,
for the attention of the entire world...

186
00:13:40,008 --> 00:13:42,218
focuses today right here in Duselheim.

187
00:13:42,302 --> 00:13:44,888
A community suddenly thrust
into prominence...

188
00:13:44,971 --> 00:13:48,892
by the unexpected discovery
of the first Wonka Golden Ticket.

189
00:13:49,058 --> 00:13:52,145
Its lucky finder is the son
of a most prominent butcher.

190
00:13:52,228 --> 00:13:54,939
The boy's name, Augustus Gloop.

191
00:13:56,566 --> 00:14:00,236
<i>The pride of Duselheim,
the fame of Western Germany.</i>

192
00:14:00,403 --> 00:14:03,239
<i>An example for the whole world.</i>

193
00:14:03,323 --> 00:14:06,159
REPORTER 2: How do you feel
to be the first Golden Ticket finder?

194
00:14:06,242 --> 00:14:08,244
AUGUSTUS: Hungry.
REPORTER 2: Any other feelings?

195
00:14:08,328 --> 00:14:11,706
I feel sorry for Wonka.
It's gonna cost him a fortune in fudge.

196
00:14:11,789 --> 00:14:13,958
REPORTER 1: Mr. Gloop,
would you mind saying-

197
00:14:14,375 --> 00:14:18,213
REPORTER 2: Would you care to say
a few words to the television audience?

198
00:14:18,296 --> 00:14:22,175
I just knew Augustus would find
a Golden Ticket. Eating is his hobby.

199
00:14:22,258 --> 00:14:23,343
MRS. GLOOP: We encourage him.

200
00:14:23,426 --> 00:14:26,471
<i>He wouldn't do it
unless he needed the nourishment.</i>

201
00:14:26,554 --> 00:14:28,723
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

202
00:14:29,224 --> 00:14:31,100
ALL: Happy birthday, Charlie!

203
00:14:33,019 --> 00:14:35,605
MRS. BUCKET: Here you are, Charlie.
CHARLIE: Thank you.

204
00:14:42,695 --> 00:14:43,780
It's terrific.

205
00:14:43,863 --> 00:14:47,242
We each knitted a bit: Grandma Georgina,
Grandma Josephine, and me.

206
00:14:47,325 --> 00:14:49,953
I did the end pieces with the little tassels.

207
00:14:50,036 --> 00:14:53,373
And here's a little gift
from Grandpa George and me.

208
00:14:53,540 --> 00:14:55,875
I think I know what this is.

209
00:14:56,876 --> 00:15:01,130
- It is. A Wonka.
- Open it. Let's see that Golden Ticket.

210
00:15:01,214 --> 00:15:03,967
- Wouldn't that be fantastic?
- Don't raise his hopes.

211
00:15:04,050 --> 00:15:07,136
Never mind. Go on, open it.
I want to see that gold.

212
00:15:07,220 --> 00:15:08,429
MRS. BUCKET: Stop it, Dad.

213
00:15:08,513 --> 00:15:10,431
<i>I've got the same chance as anybody else.</i>

214
00:15:10,515 --> 00:15:11,975
<i>GRANDPA: You've got more...</i>

215
00:15:12,058 --> 00:15:13,768
because you want it more.

216
00:15:13,852 --> 00:15:16,521
- Go on, open it.
- Here goes.

217
00:15:19,858 --> 00:15:21,609
- I got it!
- Where?

218
00:15:21,693 --> 00:15:22,944
Let's see!

219
00:15:23,027 --> 00:15:26,489
Fooled you, didn't I?
You thought I really had it.

220
00:15:28,449 --> 00:15:31,703
Never mind, Charlie. You'll find one.

221
00:15:33,037 --> 00:15:36,040
<i>- Here, everybody have a bite.
- No.</i>

222
00:15:47,927 --> 00:15:50,930
VERUCA: I wanted to be the first
to find a Golden Ticket, Daddy.

223
00:15:51,014 --> 00:15:54,267
MR. SALT: I know, angel.
We're doing the best we can.

224
00:15:54,350 --> 00:15:57,353
<i>I've got every girl on the bleeding staff
hunting for you.</i>

225
00:15:57,437 --> 00:15:59,689
Then where is it?
Why haven't they found it?

226
00:15:59,772 --> 00:16:04,694
<i>MR. SALT: Veruca, sweetheart,
I'm not a magician. Give me time!</i>

227
00:16:04,777 --> 00:16:08,031
I want it now!
What's the matter with those twerps?

228
00:16:08,114 --> 00:16:11,701
<i>For five days now,
the entire factory's been on the job!</i>

229
00:16:11,784 --> 00:16:14,621
They haven't shelled a peanut in there
since Monday!

230
00:16:14,704 --> 00:16:18,124
<i>They've been shelling
flaming chocolate bars from dawn to dusk!</i>

231
00:16:18,208 --> 00:16:19,792
Make them work nights.

232
00:16:21,753 --> 00:16:23,463
MR. SALT: Come along, you girls!

233
00:16:23,546 --> 00:16:26,841
MR. SALT: Put a jerk in it, or you'll be out
on your ears, all of you!

234
00:16:26,925 --> 00:16:30,178
And listen to this,
the first girl that finds a Golden Ticket...

235
00:16:30,261 --> 00:16:33,348
gets a £1 bonus in their pay packet!

236
00:16:33,431 --> 00:16:35,225
What do you think of that?

237
00:16:35,308 --> 00:16:36,851
[Girls cheering]

238
00:16:36,935 --> 00:16:38,436
VERUCA: They're not even trying.

239
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:41,356
They don't want to find it.
They're jealous of me.

240
00:16:41,439 --> 00:16:43,691
Sweetheart, I can't push them no harder.

241
00:16:43,775 --> 00:16:46,194
19,000 bars an hour they're shelling.

242
00:16:46,277 --> 00:16:50,031
<i>MR. SALT: 760,000 they've done so far.
VERUCA: You promised, Daddy.</i>

243
00:16:50,114 --> 00:16:52,742
<i>You promised I'd have it the very first day!</i>

244
00:16:53,034 --> 00:16:57,080
You're going to be very unpopular, Henry,
if you don't deliver soon.

245
00:16:57,163 --> 00:17:00,542
It breaks my heart, Henrietta.
I hate to see her unhappy.

246
00:17:00,625 --> 00:17:02,293
I won't talk to you ever again.

247
00:17:02,377 --> 00:17:05,797
<i>You're a rotten, mean father!
You never give me anything I want!</i>

248
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:10,510
<i>- I won't go to school till I have it.
- Veruca, sweetheart, angel.</i>

249
00:17:10,593 --> 00:17:13,346
There are only four tickets left
in the whole world...

250
00:17:13,429 --> 00:17:17,517
and the whole world's hunting for them!
What can I do?

251
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:20,687
<i>I've got it, Mr. Salt! Here it is!</i>

252
00:17:22,814 --> 00:17:25,400
VERUCA: About time, too! I want it!

253
00:17:25,483 --> 00:17:27,068
[Cheering]

254
00:17:30,655 --> 00:17:33,616
<i>VERUCA: Give me that ticket! It's mine!</i>

255
00:17:33,825 --> 00:17:36,327
I found a Golden Ticket!

256
00:17:36,536 --> 00:17:38,705
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

257
00:17:41,833 --> 00:17:43,167
Thank God.

258
00:17:44,419 --> 00:17:48,339
Happiness is what counts with children.
Happiness and harmony.

259
00:17:49,883 --> 00:17:54,012
REPORTER: This is the sign of our times.
The symbol of the havoc...

260
00:17:54,095 --> 00:17:56,931
the mad craze
that's sweeping the world today.

261
00:17:57,015 --> 00:18:01,269
Whatever corner of the globe we are in,
whichever continent we're on...

262
00:18:01,352 --> 00:18:05,231
the great search for Wonka Bars
continues. We're nearing the end...

263
00:18:05,315 --> 00:18:07,483
<i>of our 43rd day
in the hunt for Golden Tickets.</i>

264
00:18:08,776 --> 00:18:11,738
<i>And everywhere,
we're beginning to see signs of anxiety.</i>

265
00:18:11,821 --> 00:18:13,364
<i>Every hour on the hour...</i>

266
00:18:13,448 --> 00:18:16,367
<i>new shipments are being sent
to points around the globe.</i>

267
00:18:16,451 --> 00:18:18,620
<i>But they're just not moving fast enough.</i>

268
00:18:18,703 --> 00:18:23,082
<i>As time passes, the men who seek them
become more and more desperate.</i>

269
00:18:23,291 --> 00:18:26,044
<i>COMPUTER EXPERT: Gentlemen,
I know how anxious you've been...</i>

270
00:18:26,127 --> 00:18:27,545
during these last few days.

271
00:18:27,629 --> 00:18:30,006
But now I think I can safely say...

272
00:18:30,215 --> 00:18:33,092
<i>that your time and money
have been well-spent.</i>

273
00:18:33,218 --> 00:18:36,971
We're about to witness
the greatest miracle of the Machine Age.

274
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:40,183
Based on the revolutionary
law of probability...

275
00:18:40,266 --> 00:18:43,895
this machine will tell us
the precise location...

276
00:18:43,978 --> 00:18:47,106
of the three remaining Golden Tickets.

277
00:18:56,032 --> 00:19:00,078
It says,
" I won't tell. That would be cheating. "

278
00:19:06,501 --> 00:19:08,753
I am now telling the computer...

279
00:19:08,836 --> 00:19:13,049
that if it will tell me the correct answer,
I will gladly share with it...

280
00:19:13,132 --> 00:19:14,884
the grand prize.

281
00:19:19,347 --> 00:19:20,473
He says:

282
00:19:20,557 --> 00:19:24,644
" What would a computer do
with a lifetime supply of chocolate?"

283
00:19:25,103 --> 00:19:26,396
[Laughing sheepishly]

284
00:19:29,440 --> 00:19:33,987
I am now telling the computer
exactly what he can do with the chocolate.

285
00:19:36,781 --> 00:19:40,118
<i>TV ANNOUNCER: It can happen
right here, too, unbelievable as it sounds...</i>

286
00:19:40,201 --> 00:19:41,828
<i>right here in America.</i>

287
00:19:41,911 --> 00:19:46,749
<i>Where even in the smallest town,
the happiest of dreams can come true.</i>

288
00:19:46,833 --> 00:19:48,459
<i>Because, folks, here she is.:</i>

289
00:19:48,543 --> 00:19:52,255
<i>Miss Violet Beauregarde,
finder of Golden Ticket number three.</i>

290
00:19:52,380 --> 00:19:54,048
<i>From Miles City, Montana.</i>

291
00:19:54,132 --> 00:19:55,800
<i>And with her, the proud parents.</i>

292
00:19:55,884 --> 00:19:59,012
<i>Mr. Beauregarde,
a prominent local politician, a civic leader.</i>

293
00:19:59,095 --> 00:20:01,556
<i>Sam Beauregarde,
'"Square Deal'" Sam to you...</i>

294
00:20:01,639 --> 00:20:03,725
<i>with today's great giveaway bargains.</i>

295
00:20:03,808 --> 00:20:06,311
<i>The finest values you'll get
in the entire country.</i>

296
00:20:06,394 --> 00:20:08,646
<i>SAM: This number here-
VIOLET: They don't want you!</i>

297
00:20:08,730 --> 00:20:10,732
<i>- Care to say a few words?
- Sure I will.</i>

298
00:20:10,815 --> 00:20:13,276
<i>Here is Golden Ticket number three,
and it's all mine.</i>

299
00:20:13,359 --> 00:20:14,861
<i>TV ANNOUNCER: Tell us how it happened.</i>

300
00:20:14,944 --> 00:20:17,864
<i>I'm a gum chewer,
but when I heard about these tickets...</i>

301
00:20:17,989 --> 00:20:20,909
<i>I laid off gum
and switched to candy bars instead.</i>

302
00:20:20,992 --> 00:20:23,077
<i>Now, of course, I'm right back on gum.</i>

303
00:20:23,161 --> 00:20:26,247
<i>I chew all day, except at meals,
when I stick it behind my ear.</i>

304
00:20:26,331 --> 00:20:27,957
<i>MOTHER: Violet.
VIOLET: Cool it, Mother.</i>

305
00:20:28,041 --> 00:20:32,795
<i>This is a piece of gum I've been chewing
on for three months solid. A world record.</i>

306
00:20:32,879 --> 00:20:36,216
<i>It's beaten the record
held by my best friend, Cornelia...</i>

307
00:20:36,299 --> 00:20:38,134
<i>and was she mad.</i>

308
00:20:38,218 --> 00:20:40,011
<i>Cornelia, how are you, sweetie?</i>

309
00:20:40,094 --> 00:20:43,181
<i>Let me just butt in to say
if any of you folks watching...</i>

310
00:20:43,306 --> 00:20:45,975
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

311
00:20:51,648 --> 00:20:52,982
[Doorbell rings]

312
00:20:53,816 --> 00:20:55,610
Charlie, what are you doing here?

313
00:20:55,693 --> 00:20:58,029
I thought if you were ready,
I'd walk you home.

314
00:20:58,112 --> 00:21:01,366
I wish I were. Looks like
I'm going to be here late tonight.

315
00:21:01,449 --> 00:21:05,662
CHARLIE: Then I guess I'll be going.
MRS. BUCKET: Stay for a minute.

316
00:21:05,912 --> 00:21:08,998
Here. Pull up a pile of clothes
and sit down.

317
00:21:11,584 --> 00:21:13,378
Everything all right at school?

318
00:21:13,461 --> 00:21:15,255
CHARLIE: Yep.
MRS. BUCKET: Good.

319
00:21:16,422 --> 00:21:18,299
Go on your newspaper route today?

320
00:21:18,383 --> 00:21:20,635
CHARLIE: Just finished.
MRS. BUCKET: Good.

321
00:21:23,304 --> 00:21:25,390
I wanted to tell you something.

322
00:21:28,226 --> 00:21:31,396
CHARLIE: They found the third ticket today.
MRS. BUCKET: Did they?

323
00:21:34,899 --> 00:21:36,901
I guess I'll be going now.

324
00:21:38,152 --> 00:21:39,737
Is that all?

325
00:21:40,572 --> 00:21:42,532
I thought you'd like to know.

326
00:21:42,657 --> 00:21:46,286
Most people are pretty interested.
I know I'm interested.

327
00:21:46,494 --> 00:21:49,831
There are only two tickets left, you know.
Just two.

328
00:21:49,914 --> 00:21:51,624
CHARLIE: Pretty soon, just one.

329
00:21:51,708 --> 00:21:53,751
I wonder who the lucky ones will be.

330
00:21:53,835 --> 00:21:56,170
In case you're wondering, it won't be me.

331
00:21:56,254 --> 00:21:59,382
Just in case you're wondering,
you can count me out.

332
00:22:00,216 --> 00:22:02,677
There are 100 billion people in this world...

333
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:05,805
<i>and only five will find Golden Tickets.</i>

334
00:22:05,889 --> 00:22:09,434
Even if you had a sackful of money,
you probably wouldn't find one.

335
00:22:09,517 --> 00:22:14,272
After this is over, you'll be no different
from billions of others who didn't find one.

336
00:22:14,355 --> 00:22:17,400
But I am different.
I want it more than any of them.

337
00:22:18,318 --> 00:22:21,571
You'll get your chance.
One day things will change.

338
00:22:21,779 --> 00:22:24,115
When? When will they change?

339
00:22:24,365 --> 00:22:26,701
Probably when you least expect it.

340
00:22:29,954 --> 00:22:31,206
See you later.

341
00:22:38,546 --> 00:22:40,924
[Melancholic instrumental music]

342
00:22:44,677 --> 00:22:49,140
<i>[Singing] You get blue like everyone</i>

343
00:22:49,724 --> 00:22:53,019
<i>But me and Grandpa Joe</i>

344
00:22:53,102 --> 00:22:56,940
<i>Can make your troubles go away</i>

345
00:22:57,774 --> 00:22:59,817
<i>Blow away</i>

346
00:23:00,985 --> 00:23:04,197
<i>There they go</i>

347
00:23:05,573 --> 00:23:07,825
<i>Cheer up, Charlie</i>

348
00:23:09,035 --> 00:23:11,371
<i>Give me a smile</i>

349
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:16,960
<i>What happened to that smile
I used to know?</i>

350
00:23:18,753 --> 00:23:21,005
<i>Don't you know your grin</i>

351
00:23:21,089 --> 00:23:24,842
<i>Has always been my sunshine</i>

352
00:23:25,718 --> 00:23:30,598
<i>Let that sunshine show</i>

353
00:23:31,891 --> 00:23:34,102
<i>Come on, Charlie</i>

354
00:23:35,687 --> 00:23:38,565
<i>No need to frown</i>

355
00:23:38,857 --> 00:23:40,525
<i>Deep down you know</i>

356
00:23:40,859 --> 00:23:44,737
<i>The world is still your toy</i>

357
00:23:45,864 --> 00:23:48,449
<i>When the world gets heavy</i>

358
00:23:48,533 --> 00:23:51,619
<i>Never pit-a-pat 'em</i>

359
00:23:52,203 --> 00:23:56,666
<i>Up and at 'em, boy</i>

360
00:23:58,293 --> 00:24:03,339
<i>Someday, sweet as a song</i>

361
00:24:03,882 --> 00:24:08,511
<i>Charlie's lucky day will come along</i>

362
00:24:10,471 --> 00:24:15,977
<i>Till that day
You've got to stay strong, Charlie</i>

363
00:24:17,228 --> 00:24:22,859
<i>Up on top is right where you belong</i>

364
00:24:24,402 --> 00:24:27,739
<i>Look up, Charlie</i>

365
00:24:28,156 --> 00:24:31,117
<i>You'll see a star</i>

366
00:24:31,326 --> 00:24:36,497
<i>Just follow it
And keep your dream in view</i>

367
00:24:38,666 --> 00:24:44,547
<i>Pretty soon the sky
is gonna clear up, Charlie</i>

368
00:24:45,924 --> 00:24:49,928
<i>Cheer up, Charlie, do</i>

369
00:24:52,514 --> 00:24:55,183
<i>Cheer up, Charlie</i>

370
00:24:57,143 --> 00:25:03,274
<i>Just be glad you're you</i>

371
00:25:09,155 --> 00:25:10,031
[Gunshots on TV]

372
00:25:10,114 --> 00:25:12,283
<i>NEWSCASTER: While the rest of the world
searches...</i>

373
00:25:12,367 --> 00:25:14,536
<i>here in the Southwest,
it has actually happened.</i>

374
00:25:14,619 --> 00:25:18,706
That's what I said. There's only
one Golden Ticket left in the entire world.

375
00:25:18,790 --> 00:25:22,460
Because right here, in our own community
of Marble Falls, Arizona...

376
00:25:22,544 --> 00:25:24,462
is lucky winner number four.

377
00:25:24,546 --> 00:25:28,508
The name soon to be heard
around the universe is Mr. Mike Teevee.

378
00:25:28,591 --> 00:25:30,301
<i>Can we shut that thing off?</i>

379
00:25:30,385 --> 00:25:31,594
Are you crazy?

380
00:25:31,678 --> 00:25:33,304
MRS. TEEVEE: He'll answer
during the break.

381
00:25:33,388 --> 00:25:35,682
NEWSCASTER: The country
wants to hear from you.

382
00:25:35,765 --> 00:25:37,892
Can't you shut up? I'm busy.

383
00:25:37,976 --> 00:25:39,227
What a great show.

384
00:25:39,310 --> 00:25:42,939
<i>I serve all his TV dinners right here.
He's never even been to the table.</i>

385
00:25:43,022 --> 00:25:44,899
<i>- You love to watch TV?
- You bet.</i>

386
00:25:44,983 --> 00:25:48,319
- What about that Golden Ticket-
- Hold it. I want to catch this.

387
00:25:48,403 --> 00:25:49,654
REPORTER 1: Like the killings?

388
00:25:49,737 --> 00:25:51,489
What do you think life's all about?

389
00:25:51,573 --> 00:25:53,825
REPORTER 2: Mike, would you tell us if-

390
00:25:54,993 --> 00:25:57,912
Wait till I get a real one. Colt.45.

391
00:25:57,996 --> 00:26:02,250
<i>- Pop won't let me have one yet, will you?
- Not till you're 12, Son.</i>

392
00:26:04,794 --> 00:26:07,171
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

393
00:26:08,923 --> 00:26:10,800
Four down, one to go.

394
00:26:11,009 --> 00:26:15,138
And somewhere out there,
another lucky person is moving closer...

395
00:26:15,346 --> 00:26:19,809
to finding the last
of the most sought-after prizes in history.

396
00:26:20,435 --> 00:26:23,354
<i>Though we cannot help but envy him,
whoever he is...</i>

397
00:26:23,438 --> 00:26:26,524
and we might be tempted
to be bitter at our losing...

398
00:26:26,608 --> 00:26:29,527
we must remember
there are many more important things.

399
00:26:29,611 --> 00:26:32,113
Many more important things.

400
00:26:32,614 --> 00:26:36,534
Offhand, I can't think of what they are,
but I'm sure there must be something.

401
00:26:36,618 --> 00:26:38,119
Now for tomorrow's weather and...

402
00:26:40,163 --> 00:26:43,458
CHARLIE: Why did you wake me up,
Grandpa? Is something wrong?

403
00:26:49,631 --> 00:26:51,966
Grandpa, that money was for tobacco.

404
00:26:52,091 --> 00:26:55,762
<i>I told you, I've given it up. Go on, open it.</i>

405
00:26:55,970 --> 00:27:00,016
- One ticket left. Let's see that gold.
- No, you do it. I can't.

406
00:27:01,267 --> 00:27:03,978
Something tells me
we're going to be lucky this time.

407
00:27:04,062 --> 00:27:06,481
<i>I've got a funny feeling inside.</i>

408
00:27:08,316 --> 00:27:10,652
Which end shall I open first?

409
00:27:11,819 --> 00:27:14,030
That end. Just a tiny bit.

410
00:27:15,657 --> 00:27:18,243
- Like this?
- Now a bit more.

411
00:27:19,410 --> 00:27:22,413
- You finish it. I can't.
- No, Grandpa. You do it.

412
00:27:22,830 --> 00:27:25,583
All right. Here goes.

413
00:27:32,257 --> 00:27:33,675
You know...

414
00:27:34,592 --> 00:27:38,596
I bet those Golden Tickets
make the chocolate taste terrible.

415
00:27:44,936 --> 00:27:46,479
[People chattering]

416
00:27:49,315 --> 00:27:50,942
AUCTIONEER: Lot 403.

417
00:27:51,025 --> 00:27:53,570
I can personally guarantee,
ladies and gentlemen...

418
00:27:53,653 --> 00:27:57,448
that this is the one and only,
the absolutely last case of Wonka Bars...

419
00:27:57,532 --> 00:27:59,325
left in the United Kingdom.

420
00:27:59,409 --> 00:28:03,746
Shall we start the bidding at £1,000?
Do I hear £1,000?

421
00:28:03,830 --> 00:28:08,209
<i>£1,500. £2,000. I have £2,500 here.</i>

422
00:28:08,293 --> 00:28:12,755
£4,000. £4,500. £5,000...

423
00:28:13,298 --> 00:28:14,966
Your Majesty.

424
00:28:18,469 --> 00:28:20,138
DETECTIVE: I'm sorry, Mrs. Curtis.

425
00:28:20,221 --> 00:28:22,724
<i>There's nothing in these papers
to give us a clue.</i>

426
00:28:22,807 --> 00:28:24,976
They kidnapped my husband 12 hours ago.

427
00:28:25,059 --> 00:28:27,353
When will we hear from them?
What do they want?

428
00:28:27,437 --> 00:28:31,399
Try to stay calm. They did it for ransom.
We must wait to hear their demands.

429
00:28:31,482 --> 00:28:33,818
<i>I'll give them anything they want.</i>

430
00:28:33,902 --> 00:28:35,987
<i>All I want is to have Harold back.</i>

431
00:28:36,070 --> 00:28:37,614
[Phone ringing]

432
00:28:41,159 --> 00:28:42,952
DETECTIVE: Go ahead, we're listening.

433
00:28:48,249 --> 00:28:51,669
<i>What did they asked for?
Whatever it is, they can have it.</i>

434
00:28:51,753 --> 00:28:54,255
They want your case of Wonka Bars.

435
00:28:59,677 --> 00:29:01,346
<i>Did you hear me?</i>

436
00:29:02,013 --> 00:29:05,433
It's your husband's life
or your case of Wonka Bars.

437
00:29:06,100 --> 00:29:08,520
<i>How long will they give me
to think it over?</i>

438
00:29:10,021 --> 00:29:13,775
That's it, it's all over.
The Wonka contest is all over.

439
00:29:13,858 --> 00:29:16,027
The fifth and final ticket has been found.

440
00:29:16,110 --> 00:29:20,615
<i>We've got a live report coming in
directly from Paraguay, South America.</i>

441
00:29:20,698 --> 00:29:23,284
<i>Ladies and gentlemen, it is finished.
The end has come.</i>

442
00:29:23,368 --> 00:29:26,204
<i>The fifth and last Golden Ticket
has just been found...</i>

443
00:29:26,287 --> 00:29:27,789
<i>right here in Paraguay.</i>

444
00:29:27,872 --> 00:29:30,959
<i>The finder is lucky Alberto Minoleta...</i>

445
00:29:31,042 --> 00:29:34,963
<i>the multimillionaire owner of gambling
casinos throughout South America.</i>

446
00:29:35,046 --> 00:29:38,508
<i>Here is the most recent picture available
of the happy finder.</i>

447
00:29:38,591 --> 00:29:41,302
<i>- The man who has finally...
- Turn it off.</i>

448
00:29:42,720 --> 00:29:44,681
Well, that's that.

449
00:29:44,889 --> 00:29:48,810
- No more Golden Tickets.
- A lot of rubbish, the whole thing.

450
00:29:48,893 --> 00:29:50,478
Not to Charlie, it wasn't.

451
00:29:50,562 --> 00:29:55,358
A little boy needs something to hope for.
What's he got to hope for now?

452
00:29:55,567 --> 00:29:57,652
GEORGINA: Who's going to tell him?

453
00:29:57,777 --> 00:30:00,905
MRS. BUCKET: Let's not wake him.
He'll find out soon enough.

454
00:30:00,989 --> 00:30:04,659
<i>GRANDPA: Yeah, let him sleep.
Let him have one last dream.</i>

455
00:30:06,244 --> 00:30:07,829
[Slow instrumental music]

456
00:30:10,790 --> 00:30:12,625
[Turkentine clearing throat]

457
00:30:12,834 --> 00:30:15,753
<i>I've just decided to switch
our Friday's schedule to Monday.</i>

458
00:30:15,837 --> 00:30:19,424
Which means Friday's test
on what we learned during the week...

459
00:30:19,507 --> 00:30:22,093
<i>will now take place on Monday
before we've learned it.</i>

460
00:30:22,177 --> 00:30:24,929
Since today's Tuesday,
it doesn't matter in the slightest.

461
00:30:25,013 --> 00:30:26,764
TURKENTINE: Pencils ready.

462
00:30:28,183 --> 00:30:31,102
Today we are going to learn about...

463
00:30:32,562 --> 00:30:33,688
percentages.

464
00:30:33,771 --> 00:30:37,400
For example, let's take
the recent unpleasantness.

465
00:30:37,609 --> 00:30:40,820
Supposing that there were
1,000 Wonka Bars in the world...

466
00:30:40,904 --> 00:30:44,699
and during the contest you each opened
a certain number of them.

467
00:30:44,866 --> 00:30:47,035
That number is...

468
00:30:47,577 --> 00:30:49,746
a percent. Everyone understand?

469
00:30:49,829 --> 00:30:51,372
[Students moaning]

470
00:30:51,456 --> 00:30:54,542
Madeline Durkin,
how many Wonka Bars did you open?

471
00:30:54,626 --> 00:30:55,877
About 100.

472
00:30:55,960 --> 00:31:00,757
There are ten 100s in 1,000.
Therefore, you opened 10%.

473
00:31:01,382 --> 00:31:03,801
Peter Goff, how many did you open?

474
00:31:03,885 --> 00:31:05,470
One hundred and fifty.

475
00:31:05,553 --> 00:31:09,307
That's 10% half over again,
which makes 15%.

476
00:31:09,557 --> 00:31:12,852
- Charlie Bucket, how many did you open?
- Two.

477
00:31:12,977 --> 00:31:17,190
- That's easy. 200 is twice-
- Not 200.

478
00:31:17,732 --> 00:31:19,192
Just two.

479
00:31:19,400 --> 00:31:22,487
Two? What do you mean,
you only opened two?

480
00:31:22,654 --> 00:31:24,739
I don't care very much for chocolate.

481
00:31:24,822 --> 00:31:29,118
I can't figure out just two.
So let's pretend you opened 200.

482
00:31:29,244 --> 00:31:33,540
If you opened 200 Wonka Bars,
apart from being dreadfully sick...

483
00:31:33,623 --> 00:31:35,500
<i>you'd have used up 20% of 1,000...</i>

484
00:31:35,583 --> 00:31:38,503
which is 15% half over again, 10%...

485
00:31:55,353 --> 00:31:58,147
[Mysterious instrumental music]

486
00:32:06,239 --> 00:32:08,783
[Cheerful instrumental music]

487
00:32:25,884 --> 00:32:27,385
[Charlie clearing throat]

488
00:32:28,094 --> 00:32:31,222
CHARLIE: I'd like a bar of chocolate, please.
BILL: Sure.

489
00:32:31,598 --> 00:32:32,807
What kind?

490
00:32:32,891 --> 00:32:35,977
A Slugworth Sizzler?
A Wonka Scrumdiddleumptious?

491
00:32:36,060 --> 00:32:39,689
- Whichever is biggest.
- Try the Wonka Scrumdiddleumptious.

492
00:32:39,939 --> 00:32:44,194
<i>Now that the tickets have been found,
I don't have to hide them anymore.</i>

493
00:32:47,655 --> 00:32:49,240
[Bill clearing throat]

494
00:32:56,080 --> 00:32:57,582
Take it easy.

495
00:32:57,665 --> 00:33:00,335
You'll get a stomachache
if you swallow it like that.

496
00:33:00,418 --> 00:33:02,295
- Bye.
- Bye, now.

497
00:33:10,094 --> 00:33:13,431
I think I'll buy just one more,
for my Grandpa Joe.

498
00:33:13,640 --> 00:33:14,933
Sure.

499
00:33:15,683 --> 00:33:18,019
Why not try
a regular Wonka Bar this time?

500
00:33:18,102 --> 00:33:19,437
CHARLIE: Fine.

501
00:33:23,608 --> 00:33:25,193
[People talking excitedly]

502
00:33:27,529 --> 00:33:30,490
<i>MR. JOPECK: Hear about the scandal.
MAN 1: Give me a newspaper.</i>

503
00:33:30,573 --> 00:33:32,242
MR. JOPECK: All right.

504
00:33:32,325 --> 00:33:35,662
MR. JOPECK: Take it easy. One at a time.
MAN 2: Did you hear the news?

505
00:33:35,745 --> 00:33:38,122
MAN 3: That gambler from Paraguay
made a phony ticket.

506
00:33:38,206 --> 00:33:41,459
MAN 2: That means there's
one Golden Ticket still floating around.

507
00:33:41,543 --> 00:33:45,255
<i>MAN 3: Can you imagine the nerve
of that guy? Trying to fool the whole world.</i>

508
00:33:45,338 --> 00:33:49,175
MAN 2: He really was a crook.
This means the contest goes on forever.

509
00:34:02,063 --> 00:34:04,524
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

510
00:34:24,002 --> 00:34:26,921
[Triumphant instrumental music]

511
00:34:32,427 --> 00:34:35,138
<i>You've got it!
You've got the last Golden Ticket!</i>

512
00:34:35,221 --> 00:34:38,057
The kid's found the last Golden Ticket!

513
00:34:39,475 --> 00:34:41,019
MAN 4: It really is gold.

514
00:34:41,102 --> 00:34:44,147
<i>MR. JOPECK: Stand back. Leave him alone.
WOMAN: Let me see.</i>

515
00:34:44,230 --> 00:34:46,274
<i>MR. JOPECK: You'll kill him.
Leave him alone.</i>

516
00:34:46,357 --> 00:34:48,860
MR. JOPECK: Break it up!
MAN 5: Show it over here.

517
00:34:48,943 --> 00:34:51,487
MR. JOPECK: Come on, Charlie.
Hold on to the ticket.

518
00:34:51,571 --> 00:34:55,992
MR. JOPECK: Run for it! Run straight home,
and don't stop till you get there!

519
00:34:56,159 --> 00:34:58,620
[Fast-paced instrumental music]

520
00:35:25,438 --> 00:35:27,732
[Ominous instrumental music]

521
00:35:27,941 --> 00:35:30,985
I congratulate you, little boy. Well done.

522
00:35:31,444 --> 00:35:33,905
<i>You've found the fifth Golden Ticket.</i>

523
00:35:34,113 --> 00:35:37,367
May I introduce myself? Arthur Slugworth.

524
00:35:37,951 --> 00:35:40,745
President of Slugworth Chocolates, Inc.

525
00:35:41,329 --> 00:35:45,250
Listen carefully,
because I'm going to make you very rich.

526
00:35:45,458 --> 00:35:49,170
Mr. Wonka is at this moment
working on a fantastic invention.

527
00:35:49,671 --> 00:35:51,965
The Everlasting Gobstopper.

528
00:35:52,632 --> 00:35:54,759
If he succeeds, he'll ruin me.

529
00:35:54,843 --> 00:35:58,263
So I want you to get hold of
just one Everlasting Gobstopper...

530
00:35:58,346 --> 00:36:01,933
and bring it to me
so that I can find the secret formula.

531
00:36:02,016 --> 00:36:05,103
Your reward will be 10,000 of these.

532
00:36:05,812 --> 00:36:09,649
Think it over, will you?
A new house for your family...

533
00:36:09,858 --> 00:36:13,152
and good food and comfort
for the rest of their lives.

534
00:36:13,987 --> 00:36:15,905
And don't forget the name:

535
00:36:15,989 --> 00:36:18,199
Everlasting Gobstopper.

536
00:36:27,834 --> 00:36:30,545
[Cheerful instrumental music]

537
00:36:47,645 --> 00:36:51,316
<i>Look, everybody!
I've got it! The fifth Golden Ticket is mine!</i>

538
00:36:51,399 --> 00:36:55,987
You're pulling our legs, Charlie.
There aren't any more Golden Tickets.

539
00:36:56,070 --> 00:36:59,324
No, Grandpa, the last one was a fake.
It said so in the papers.

540
00:36:59,407 --> 00:37:02,994
I found some money, bought a Wonka Bar,
and the ticket was in it!

541
00:37:03,077 --> 00:37:05,413
Look at it. See for yourself.

542
00:37:06,080 --> 00:37:08,541
Read it, Joe, for heaven's sake.

543
00:37:09,083 --> 00:37:13,379
" Greetings to you,
the lucky finder of this Golden Ticket...

544
00:37:13,588 --> 00:37:17,675
" from Mr. Willy Wonka.
Present this ticket at the factory gates...

545
00:37:17,759 --> 00:37:22,013
" at 10:00 in the morning on October 1,
and do not be late.

546
00:37:22,222 --> 00:37:27,018
" You may bring with you one member
of your own family, but no one else.

547
00:37:27,227 --> 00:37:30,355
" In your wildest dreams,
you could not imagine...

548
00:37:30,438 --> 00:37:33,149
" the marvelous surprises that await you. "

549
00:37:33,233 --> 00:37:36,361
<i>GRANDPA: Charlie, you've done it!
MRS. BUCKET: I can't believe it.</i>

550
00:37:37,237 --> 00:37:41,282
It says I can take somebody with me.
I wish you could go.

551
00:37:59,092 --> 00:38:00,176
[Grandpa groans]

552
00:38:00,260 --> 00:38:03,429
That's good, Charlie. Now help me up.

553
00:38:17,277 --> 00:38:20,655
CHARLIE: Are you okay?
GRANDPA: I'm fine, Charlie.

554
00:38:25,869 --> 00:38:26,953
[Charlie exclaiming]

555
00:38:27,036 --> 00:38:28,037
MRS. BUCKET: Easy, Dad.
JOSEPHINE: Joe!

556
00:38:30,665 --> 00:38:32,166
JOSEPHINE: Watch it, Joe.

557
00:38:41,342 --> 00:38:42,844
Look at me.

558
00:38:45,722 --> 00:38:47,307
Look at me!

559
00:38:48,349 --> 00:38:50,351
Up and about.

560
00:38:51,978 --> 00:38:55,607
I haven't done this in 20 years.

561
00:38:56,399 --> 00:38:57,859
Grandpa.

562
00:39:01,821 --> 00:39:05,700
<i>[Singing] I never thought my life could be</i>

563
00:39:06,826 --> 00:39:10,705
<i>Anything but catastrophe</i>

564
00:39:11,497 --> 00:39:15,168
<i>But suddenly I begin to see</i>

565
00:39:15,835 --> 00:39:20,089
<i>A bit of good luck for me</i>

566
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:24,844
<i>'Cause I've got a Golden Ticket</i>

567
00:39:26,429 --> 00:39:32,560
<i>I've got a golden twinkle in my eye</i>

568
00:39:36,689 --> 00:39:39,567
<i>I never had a chance to shine</i>

569
00:39:39,651 --> 00:39:41,819
<i>Never a happy song to sing</i>

570
00:39:41,903 --> 00:39:43,947
<i>But suddenly half the world is mine</i>

571
00:39:44,030 --> 00:39:45,615
<i>What an amazing thing!</i>

572
00:39:45,823 --> 00:39:47,784
<i>'Cause I've got a Golden Ticket</i>

573
00:39:47,867 --> 00:39:49,661
It's ours, Charlie.

574
00:39:49,869 --> 00:39:53,623
<i>[Singing] I've got a golden sun up in the sky</i>

575
00:39:53,706 --> 00:39:55,375
Slippers, Charlie.

576
00:39:55,917 --> 00:39:57,669
[Lively instrumental music]

577
00:39:57,752 --> 00:40:01,339
<i>[Singing] I never thought I'd see the day
When I would face the world and say</i>

578
00:40:01,422 --> 00:40:04,509
<i>GRANDPA AND CHARLIE: '"Good morning,
look at the sun '"</i>

579
00:40:05,093 --> 00:40:08,638
<i>GRANDPA: I never thought that I would be
Slap in the lap of luxury</i>

580
00:40:08,721 --> 00:40:10,515
<i>Because I'd have said.:</i>

581
00:40:10,598 --> 00:40:12,141
<i>CHARLIE: '"It couldn't be done'"</i>

582
00:40:12,350 --> 00:40:14,102
<i>GRANDPA: But it can be done</i>

583
00:40:15,687 --> 00:40:17,689
The cane, Charlie.

584
00:40:20,733 --> 00:40:22,360
[Grandpa laughing]

585
00:40:22,443 --> 00:40:24,028
GRANDPA: Here I go.

586
00:40:24,946 --> 00:40:26,781
[Laughing] Watch my speed.

587
00:40:32,078 --> 00:40:33,288
[Grandpa singing]

588
00:40:33,371 --> 00:40:37,041
<i>I never dreamed that I would climb
Over the moon in ecstasy</i>

589
00:40:37,125 --> 00:40:40,753
<i>But nevertheless it's there
That I'm shortly about to be</i>

590
00:40:40,837 --> 00:40:43,047
<i>GRANDPA AND CHARLIE:
'Cause I've got a Golden Ticket</i>

591
00:40:44,382 --> 00:40:48,178
<i>I've got a golden chance to make my way</i>

592
00:40:48,386 --> 00:40:53,224
<i>And with a Golden Ticket, it's a golden day</i>

593
00:40:56,603 --> 00:40:58,855
[Lively instrumental music continues]

594
00:41:09,866 --> 00:41:12,869
Good morning! Look at the sun!

595
00:41:17,165 --> 00:41:20,919
<i>GRANDPA AND CHARLIE: [Singing]
Because I'd have said it couldn't be done</i>

596
00:41:21,252 --> 00:41:23,922
<i>But it can be done</i>

597
00:41:24,589 --> 00:41:28,593
<i>I never dreamed that I would climb
Over the moon in ecstasy</i>

598
00:41:28,676 --> 00:41:32,555
<i>But nevertheless it's there
That I'm shortly about to be</i>

599
00:41:32,764 --> 00:41:35,266
<i>'Cause I've got a Golden Ticket</i>

600
00:41:35,600 --> 00:41:37,602
<i>GRANDPA AND CHARLIE:
I've got a Golden Ticket</i>

601
00:41:37,685 --> 00:41:41,314
<i>I've got a golden chance to make my way</i>

602
00:41:41,523 --> 00:41:46,361
<i>And with a Golden Ticket it's a golden day</i>

603
00:41:50,365 --> 00:41:53,868
Stop! It says October 1. That's tomorrow.

604
00:41:53,952 --> 00:41:58,248
<i>We've got a lot to do. Comb your hair,
wash your face, polish your shoes-</i>

605
00:41:58,331 --> 00:42:00,166
MRS. BUCKET: I'll take care of everything.

606
00:42:00,250 --> 00:42:01,376
<i>We don't have time.</i>

607
00:42:01,459 --> 00:42:05,630
Grandpa, on the way home today,
I ran into Mr. Slugworth.

608
00:42:07,549 --> 00:42:10,051
[Marching band
instrumental music playing]

609
00:42:30,321 --> 00:42:32,156
Hey, Mom, we're on TV.

610
00:42:32,240 --> 00:42:34,325
Hi, everybody in Marble Falls!

611
00:42:34,409 --> 00:42:38,246
Hi, Billy. Hi, Maggie. Hi, Fishface.
How do I look?

612
00:42:38,329 --> 00:42:39,914
ANNOUNCER: You guys ready?
MAN: You're on.

613
00:42:39,998 --> 00:42:41,791
This is the big day, folks.

614
00:42:41,875 --> 00:42:46,754
<i>The day Willy Wonka will open his gates
and shower gifts on the five lucky winners.</i>

615
00:42:46,838 --> 00:42:50,758
<i>From everywhere, people have gathered
waiting for the hour to strike...</i>

616
00:42:50,842 --> 00:42:54,512
waiting to catch a glimpse
of that legendary magician, Willy Wonka.

617
00:42:56,181 --> 00:42:59,809
Hi, Sam Beauregarde here. Don't forget
to visit Beauregarde's Auto Mart-

618
00:42:59,893 --> 00:43:02,854
Cut it out, Dad!
For heaven's sake, this is my show.

619
00:43:02,937 --> 00:43:06,774
<i>Hi, Cornelia, sweetie. I've still got it.
How's this for a stretch?</i>

620
00:43:11,487 --> 00:43:12,906
[People chattering]

621
00:43:19,954 --> 00:43:24,292
- I want to go in first before anybody else.
- Anything you say, sweetheart.

622
00:43:27,545 --> 00:43:30,965
<i>Save some room for later,
Augustus, Liebling.</i>

623
00:43:33,885 --> 00:43:37,722
<i>[Whispering] Grandpa, I don't believe it.
We did it. We're actually going in.</i>

624
00:43:37,805 --> 00:43:41,559
We're going to see
the greatest of them all, Mr. Willy Wonka.

625
00:43:41,809 --> 00:43:43,895
[Clock dinging]

626
00:44:08,670 --> 00:44:10,839
[People cheering and applauding]

627
00:45:17,614 --> 00:45:19,657
[People cheering]

628
00:45:22,911 --> 00:45:24,245
WONKA: Thank you.

629
00:45:28,833 --> 00:45:32,378
Welcome, my friends.
Welcome to my chocolate factory.

630
00:45:33,004 --> 00:45:35,215
Would you come forward, please?

631
00:45:35,423 --> 00:45:38,343
Get back, you.
Come on, Veruca, sweetheart.

632
00:45:39,344 --> 00:45:41,262
[Cheering]

633
00:45:43,473 --> 00:45:46,059
[Whispering] That's Slugworth,
the man I told you about.

634
00:45:50,563 --> 00:45:53,358
<i>WONKA: Welcome.
It's nice to have you here.</i>

635
00:45:53,441 --> 00:45:55,318
I'm so glad you could come.

636
00:45:55,401 --> 00:45:59,322
This is going to be such an exciting day.
I hope you enjoy it.

637
00:45:59,739 --> 00:46:01,241
I think you will.

638
00:46:01,449 --> 00:46:04,661
And now would you please show me
your Golden Tickets?

639
00:46:04,827 --> 00:46:06,329
I'm Veruca Salt.

640
00:46:06,412 --> 00:46:08,540
My dear Veruca, what a pleasure.

641
00:46:08,623 --> 00:46:12,794
<i>- You look pretty in that lovely mink coat.
- I've got three others at home.</i>

642
00:46:12,877 --> 00:46:17,215
Mr. Salt, overjoyed to see you, sir.
Would you just step over there?

643
00:46:17,423 --> 00:46:20,718
- Augustus Gloop.
- Augustus, my dear boy.

644
00:46:20,802 --> 00:46:23,471
How good to see you,
and in such fine shape.

645
00:46:23,596 --> 00:46:26,975
This must be the radiant Mrs. Gloop.
Just over there, dear lady.

646
00:46:27,058 --> 00:46:28,393
Violet Beauregarde.

647
00:46:28,476 --> 00:46:30,937
WONKA: Darling child.
Welcome to Wonka's.

648
00:46:31,020 --> 00:46:34,357
VIOLET: What kind of gum you got here?
WONKA: Charming.

649
00:46:34,440 --> 00:46:37,610
- Sam Beauregarde here.
- My dear sir, what a genuine pleasure.

650
00:46:37,694 --> 00:46:40,864
<i>Any automotive needs, call on Sam.
Phone number's on the card.</i>

651
00:46:40,947 --> 00:46:43,032
" With Sam B., it's a guarantee. "

652
00:46:44,117 --> 00:46:45,743
I'm Mike Teevee.

653
00:46:45,827 --> 00:46:49,038
MIKE: Wham, you're dead!
WONKA: Wonderful to meet you, Mike.

654
00:46:49,122 --> 00:46:50,915
WONKA: Mrs. Teevee, how do you do?

655
00:46:50,999 --> 00:46:53,084
- What an adorable boy you have.
- Thank you.

656
00:46:53,167 --> 00:46:55,461
- Just over there.
- Charlie Bucket.

657
00:46:55,545 --> 00:47:00,133
WONKA: I read about you in
the papers. I'm happy for you. Who's this?

658
00:47:00,216 --> 00:47:03,178
CHARLIE: My grandfather, Grandpa Joe.
WONKA: Delighted to meet you.

659
00:47:03,261 --> 00:47:05,305
Overjoyed. Enraptured. Entranced.

660
00:47:05,388 --> 00:47:07,974
Are we ready? Yes. Good. In we go.

661
00:47:08,474 --> 00:47:10,977
[Marching band instrumental music
resumes playing]

662
00:47:44,010 --> 00:47:46,346
Hats, coats, galoshes over here.

663
00:47:46,513 --> 00:47:49,682
<i>But hurry, please.
We have so much time and so little to see.</i>

664
00:47:49,766 --> 00:47:51,726
Wait a minute. Strike that.

665
00:47:51,935 --> 00:47:54,020
Reverse it. Thank you.

666
00:47:55,897 --> 00:47:59,609
VIOLET: When do I get my chocolate?
SAM: First take off your coat, Violet.

667
00:47:59,692 --> 00:48:02,278
MIKE: Boy, what weird-looking
coat hangers.

668
00:48:02,529 --> 00:48:03,613
[Violet and Mike scream]

669
00:48:03,696 --> 00:48:08,117
Little surprises around every corner,
but nothing dangerous. Don't be alarmed.

670
00:48:08,451 --> 00:48:12,163
<i>As soon as your outer vestments
are in hand, we'll begin.</i>

671
00:48:13,164 --> 00:48:17,335
Now, will the children kindly step up here.

672
00:48:28,388 --> 00:48:30,557
VIOLET: Accidents? What kind
of accidents?

673
00:48:30,640 --> 00:48:33,142
MRS. TEEVEE: I didn't know
we had to sign for this tour.

674
00:48:33,226 --> 00:48:35,353
VIOLET: I can't see what it says
in the bottom.

675
00:48:35,436 --> 00:48:37,313
Violet, you first.

676
00:48:37,564 --> 00:48:39,899
WONKA: Sign here.
SAM: Hold it.

677
00:48:39,983 --> 00:48:42,360
SAM: Let me through.
Violet, don't sign anything.

678
00:48:42,443 --> 00:48:45,572
- What's this all about?
- Standard formal contract.

679
00:48:45,655 --> 00:48:49,492
Don't talk to me about contracts,
I use them myself. They're for suckers.

680
00:48:49,576 --> 00:48:52,829
You wouldn't begrudge me
a little protection? A drop?

681
00:48:52,912 --> 00:48:54,622
I don't sign anything without my lawyer.

682
00:48:54,706 --> 00:48:56,499
MR. SALT: Veruca
don't sign anything, either.

683
00:48:56,583 --> 00:48:57,667
Then she don't go in.

684
00:48:57,750 --> 00:48:59,252
I'm sorry. Rules of the house.

685
00:48:59,335 --> 00:49:01,713
I want to go in. Don't you dare stop me.

686
00:49:01,796 --> 00:49:03,423
MR. SALT: I'm only trying to help you.

687
00:49:03,506 --> 00:49:07,635
<i>Give me that pen.
You're always making things difficult.</i>

688
00:49:08,928 --> 00:49:12,599
Nicely handled, Veruca.
She's a girl who knows where she's going.

689
00:49:13,391 --> 00:49:15,852
What's all that small print at the bottom?

690
00:49:15,935 --> 00:49:19,189
<i>If you have any problems,
dial information. Thank you for calling.</i>

691
00:49:22,275 --> 00:49:24,944
MRS. TEEVEE: I assume there's
an accident indemnity clause?

692
00:49:25,028 --> 00:49:26,196
Never between friends.

693
00:49:26,279 --> 00:49:30,241
Saw this in a movie once.
A guy signed his wife's insurance policy.

694
00:49:30,325 --> 00:49:32,202
- Then he bumped her off.
- Clever.

695
00:49:32,285 --> 00:49:35,914
<i>- What about me, Grandpa?
- Sign away. We've got nothing to lose.</i>

696
00:49:35,997 --> 00:49:37,624
VERUCA: Let's go in. Come on.

697
00:49:37,707 --> 00:49:41,002
Patience, little dear.
Everything has to be in order.

698
00:49:41,503 --> 00:49:43,505
Everyone signed? Yes?

699
00:49:43,671 --> 00:49:45,507
Good. On we go.

700
00:49:54,682 --> 00:49:56,267
WONKA: Ninety-nine...

701
00:49:56,476 --> 00:49:57,936
forty-four...

702
00:49:58,019 --> 00:49:59,938
100% pure.

703
00:50:01,523 --> 00:50:03,775
Just through the other door, please.

704
00:50:06,694 --> 00:50:10,281
MR. SALT: There's some mistake here.
MIKE: There is no other door.

705
00:50:10,532 --> 00:50:14,369
- There's no way out.
- I know there's a door here someplace.

706
00:50:14,869 --> 00:50:18,331
SAM: I don't like this, Wonka.
MR. SALT: Is this a trick or something?

707
00:50:18,456 --> 00:50:22,126
<i>Mr. Wonka, help!
I'm getting squashed. Save me.</i>

708
00:50:22,210 --> 00:50:24,546
Is it my soul that calls upon my name?

709
00:50:24,629 --> 00:50:26,464
Let me out or I'll scream!

710
00:50:26,548 --> 00:50:29,050
- Somebody's touching me.
- Now look here, Wonka!

711
00:50:29,133 --> 00:50:33,721
Excuse me, questions will come at the end of the session.
We must press on. Come along.

712
00:50:35,139 --> 00:50:37,100
WONKA: Here we are.
SAM: Ugh, don't be a damn fool. Wonka!

713
00:50:37,183 --> 00:50:38,852
That's the way we came in.

714
00:50:38,935 --> 00:50:41,062
It is? Are you sure?

715
00:50:41,229 --> 00:50:43,314
<i>We've just come through there.</i>

716
00:50:43,690 --> 00:50:45,275
How do you like that?

717
00:50:46,150 --> 00:50:48,444
WONKA: There we are.
MR. SALT: What is this, Wonka?

718
00:50:48,528 --> 00:50:51,364
- Some kind of funhouse?
- Why? Having fun?

719
00:50:51,447 --> 00:50:55,535
MRS. TEEVEE: I'm had enough!
I'm not going in there.
MR. BEAUREGARDE: Come on, Violet, we're getting out of here.

720
00:50:55,618 --> 00:50:59,122
You can't get out backwards.
You got to go forwards to go back.

721
00:50:59,205 --> 00:51:00,707
Better press on.

722
00:51:01,708 --> 00:51:04,294
MIKE: The room is getting smaller.
MRS. TEEVEE: No, it's not.

723
00:51:04,377 --> 00:51:07,046
MRS. TEEVEE: He's getting bigger.
MR. SALT: He's at it again.

724
00:51:07,130 --> 00:51:09,966
VIOLET: Where's the chocolate?
SAM: I doubt if there is any.

725
00:51:10,049 --> 00:51:12,427
I doubt if any of us
will get out of here alive.

726
00:51:12,510 --> 00:51:15,013
Oh, you should never never doubt
what nobody is sure about.

727
00:51:15,096 --> 00:51:17,390
You're not squeezing me
through that tiny door.

728
00:51:17,473 --> 00:51:20,226
You're off your nut.
No one can get through there.

729
00:51:20,310 --> 00:51:24,647
<i>My dear friends, you are now
about to enter the nerve center...</i>

730
00:51:24,981 --> 00:51:27,233
to the entire Wonka factory.

731
00:51:28,151 --> 00:51:31,946
Inside this room,
all of my dreams become realities.

732
00:51:32,155 --> 00:51:34,657
And some of my realities become dreams.

733
00:51:34,741 --> 00:51:38,494
And almost everything you'll see
is eatable, edible.

734
00:51:38,578 --> 00:51:40,788
I mean, you can eat almost everything.

735
00:51:40,872 --> 00:51:42,665
Let me in, I'm starving.

736
00:51:42,749 --> 00:51:45,877
Don't get overexcited.
Don't lose your head, Augustus.

737
00:51:45,960 --> 00:51:47,587
We wouldn't want anyone to lose that.

738
00:51:49,839 --> 00:51:50,965
Yet.

739
00:51:51,174 --> 00:51:52,967
WONKA: Now, the combination.

740
00:51:53,176 --> 00:51:55,178
This is a musical lock.

741
00:51:55,261 --> 00:51:57,514
[Lively piano music playing]

742
00:51:57,680 --> 00:51:58,890
Rachmaninoff.

743
00:51:59,098 --> 00:52:01,059
WONKA: Ladies and gentlemen...

744
00:52:01,518 --> 00:52:03,186
boys and girls...

745
00:52:04,187 --> 00:52:05,939
the Chocolate Room.

746
00:52:06,648 --> 00:52:10,109
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

747
00:52:43,935 --> 00:52:46,521
WONKA: Hold your breath. Make a wish.

748
00:52:46,771 --> 00:52:48,314
<i>Count to three.</i>

749
00:52:48,982 --> 00:52:52,485
<i>[Singing] Come with me and you'll be</i>

750
00:52:52,694 --> 00:52:56,364
<i>In a world of pure imagination</i>

751
00:52:56,906 --> 00:52:58,366
<i>Take a look</i>

752
00:52:58,575 --> 00:53:03,663
<i>And you'll see into your imagination</i>

753
00:53:10,003 --> 00:53:13,840
<i>We'll begin with a spin</i>

754
00:53:14,215 --> 00:53:17,927
<i>Traveling in the world of my creation</i>

755
00:53:18,261 --> 00:53:21,973
<i>What we'll see will defy</i>

756
00:53:23,183 --> 00:53:25,435
<i>Explanation</i>

757
00:53:27,979 --> 00:53:29,564
[Slow instrumental music]

758
00:53:35,820 --> 00:53:39,282
<i>[Singing] If you want to view paradise</i>

759
00:53:39,657 --> 00:53:42,869
<i>Simply look around and view it</i>

760
00:53:44,037 --> 00:53:47,123
<i>Anything you want to, do it</i>

761
00:53:48,124 --> 00:53:50,502
<i>Want to change the world</i>

762
00:53:51,753 --> 00:53:54,130
<i>There's nothing to it</i>

763
00:54:02,597 --> 00:54:05,266
SAM: Hurry up, Violet.
CHARLIE: This way, Grandpa.

764
00:54:12,148 --> 00:54:14,984
<i>[Singing] There is no life i know</i>

765
00:54:15,068 --> 00:54:18,238
<i>To compare with pure imagination</i>

766
00:54:18,321 --> 00:54:21,199
<i>Living there you'll be free</i>

767
00:54:21,616 --> 00:54:25,495
<i>If you truly wish to be</i>

768
00:55:06,661 --> 00:55:10,248
<i>If you want to view paradise</i>

769
00:55:10,707 --> 00:55:13,376
<i>Simply look around and view it</i>

770
00:55:14,335 --> 00:55:17,839
<i>Anything you want to, do it</i>

771
00:55:18,381 --> 00:55:20,967
<i>Want to change the world</i>

772
00:55:21,134 --> 00:55:25,430
<i>There's nothing to it</i>

773
00:55:37,150 --> 00:55:41,070
<i>There is no life I know</i>

774
00:55:42,572 --> 00:55:47,368
<i>To compare with pure imagination</i>

775
00:55:48,828 --> 00:55:53,958
<i>Living there you'll be free</i>

776
00:55:55,418 --> 00:55:59,005
<i>If you truly</i>

777
00:56:00,840 --> 00:56:04,761
<i>Wish to be</i>

778
00:56:16,105 --> 00:56:18,525
MRS. GLOOP: What a disgusting,
dirty river.

779
00:56:18,608 --> 00:56:19,943
It's industrial waste.

780
00:56:20,026 --> 00:56:22,445
<i>You've ruined your watershed, Wonka.
It's polluted.</i>

781
00:56:22,529 --> 00:56:25,114
- It's chocolate.
- That's chocolate?

782
00:56:25,198 --> 00:56:26,533
That's chocolate.

783
00:56:26,616 --> 00:56:28,201
A chocolate river.

784
00:56:28,284 --> 00:56:30,620
<i>GRANDPA: That's the most fantastic thing
I've ever seen.</i>

785
00:56:30,703 --> 00:56:32,539
WONKA: 10,000 gallons an hour.

786
00:56:32,789 --> 00:56:36,543
And look at my waterfall.
That's the most important thing.

787
00:56:37,627 --> 00:56:41,422
<i>It's mixing my chocolate.
It's actually churning my chocolate.</i>

788
00:56:41,631 --> 00:56:45,385
No other factory in the world
mixes its chocolate by waterfall.

789
00:56:45,802 --> 00:56:49,055
But it's the only way
if you want it just right.

790
00:56:49,222 --> 00:56:53,059
Grandpa, look over there across the river.
They're little men.

791
00:56:53,142 --> 00:56:55,061
GRANDPA: Jumping crocodiles, Charlie!

792
00:56:55,186 --> 00:56:57,397
Now we know who makes the chocolates.

793
00:56:57,480 --> 00:57:00,650
MR. SALT: I never saw anybody
with an orange face before.

794
00:57:01,276 --> 00:57:04,946
MR. SALT: Aren't they funny-looking?
MRS. TEEVEE: What are they doing there?

795
00:57:05,029 --> 00:57:06,990
WONKA: Creaming and sugaring time.

796
00:57:07,073 --> 00:57:10,326
VIOLET: They can't be real people.
WONKA: Of course they are.

797
00:57:10,410 --> 00:57:11,661
MR. SALT: Stuff and nonsense!

798
00:57:11,744 --> 00:57:13,454
No, Oompa Loompas.

799
00:57:14,789 --> 00:57:17,917
- From Loompaland.
- Loompaland? There's no such place.

800
00:57:18,001 --> 00:57:20,962
- Excuse me, dear lady-
- I am a geography teacher.

801
00:57:21,045 --> 00:57:24,424
Then you know all about it
and what a terrible country it is.

802
00:57:24,507 --> 00:57:27,635
Nothing but desolate wastes
and fierce beasts.

803
00:57:27,844 --> 00:57:31,014
The poor little Oompa Loompas
were so small and helpless...

804
00:57:31,097 --> 00:57:33,016
they would get gobbled up right and left.

805
00:57:33,099 --> 00:57:37,395
A Wangdoodle would eat 10 of them
for breakfast and think nothing of it.

806
00:57:37,520 --> 00:57:38,855
And so I said:

807
00:57:39,189 --> 00:57:41,983
<i>" Come and live with me
in peace and safety...</i>

808
00:57:42,192 --> 00:57:45,195
" away from all the Wangdoodles
and Hornswogglers...

809
00:57:45,278 --> 00:57:48,781
" and Snozzwangers
and rotten Vermicious Knids. "

810
00:57:48,865 --> 00:57:52,911
Snozzwangers? Vermicious Knids?
What kind of rubbish is that?

811
00:57:52,994 --> 00:57:55,872
I'm sorry, but all questions
must be submitted in writing.

812
00:57:55,955 --> 00:57:58,041
So, in the greatest of secrecy...

813
00:57:58,124 --> 00:58:01,794
I transported the entire population
of Oompa Loompas to my factory.

814
00:58:01,878 --> 00:58:05,882
I want an Oompa Loompa.
I want you to get me one right away.

815
00:58:05,965 --> 00:58:08,843
All right, I'll get you one
before the day's out.

816
00:58:08,968 --> 00:58:11,304
I want an Oompa Loompa now.

817
00:58:11,387 --> 00:58:12,889
VIOLET: Can it, you nit!

818
00:58:13,431 --> 00:58:15,183
AUGUSTUS: This stuff is terrific.

819
00:58:15,683 --> 00:58:18,895
CHARLIE: Look at Augustus.
GRANDPA: Don't worry, he can't drink it all.

820
00:58:19,312 --> 00:58:22,899
<i>Augustus, sweetheart,
save some room for later.</i>

821
00:58:23,149 --> 00:58:27,153
Please don't do that. My chocolate
must never be touched by human hands.

822
00:58:27,695 --> 00:58:30,740
Don't do that!
You're contaminating my entire river.

823
00:58:30,823 --> 00:58:32,992
Please, I beg you! Augustus!

824
00:58:33,076 --> 00:58:34,327
[Exclaiming]

825
00:58:34,410 --> 00:58:36,329
WONKA: My chocolate!
AUGUSTUS: Help!

826
00:58:37,997 --> 00:58:40,375
My chocolate. My beautiful chocolate.

827
00:58:41,626 --> 00:58:44,170
MRS. GLOOP: Don't just stand there.
Do something!

828
00:58:44,254 --> 00:58:46,506
Help. Police. Murder.

829
00:58:46,589 --> 00:58:49,843
GRANDPA: Quick, Charlie. Here.
CHARLIE: Quick, Augustus, grab this.

830
00:58:50,301 --> 00:58:51,761
MRS. TEEVEE: What's happening to him?

831
00:58:51,845 --> 00:58:54,180
<i>MR. SALT: He's drowning.
MRS. GLOOP: Dive in. Save him!</i>

832
00:58:54,264 --> 00:58:55,348
It's too late.

833
00:58:55,431 --> 00:58:58,059
- Too late?
- He's had it now. The suction's got him.

834
00:58:58,142 --> 00:58:59,936
MRS. GLOOP: Augustus, come back!

835
00:59:00,019 --> 00:59:02,522
- Where is he?
- Watch the pipe.

836
00:59:03,022 --> 00:59:05,233
VERUCA: How long is he going to
stay down, Daddy?

837
00:59:05,316 --> 00:59:08,278
MRS. GLOOP: He can't swim.
WONKA: There's no better time to learn.

838
00:59:08,361 --> 00:59:11,364
MIKE: His coat's going up the pipe.
SAM: Call a plumber.

839
00:59:11,447 --> 00:59:13,533
MR. SALT: He's stuck in the pipe, isn't he?

840
00:59:13,616 --> 00:59:16,035
- It's his stomach that's done that.
- Help!

841
00:59:16,119 --> 00:59:19,038
VIOLET: He's blocking the chocolate.
GRANDPA: What happens now?

842
00:59:19,122 --> 00:59:23,376
The pressure will get him out.
It's building up behind the blockage.

843
00:59:24,002 --> 00:59:26,713
MR. SALT: How long is it going to take
to push through?

844
00:59:26,796 --> 00:59:30,258
The suspense is terrible. I hope it'll last.

845
00:59:30,341 --> 00:59:31,634
MR. SALT: Go on, boy.

846
00:59:33,386 --> 00:59:36,389
CHARLIE: He'll never get out.
GRANDPA: Yes, he will. Watch.

847
00:59:36,472 --> 00:59:39,851
Remember you once asked me
how a bullet comes out of a gun?

848
00:59:40,768 --> 00:59:43,313
<i>He'll be made into marshmallows
in five seconds.</i>

849
00:59:43,396 --> 00:59:46,733
- Impossible. That's absurd, unthinkable.
- Why?

850
00:59:47,066 --> 00:59:49,569
Because that pipe goes
to the Fudge Room.

851
00:59:49,652 --> 00:59:51,321
MRS. GLOOP: You terrible man.

852
00:59:53,740 --> 00:59:56,492
Take Mrs. Gloop to the Fudge Room,
but look sharp...

853
00:59:56,576 --> 00:59:58,786
or her little boy will get poured into the boiler.

854
00:59:58,870 --> 01:00:00,622
You boiled him up, I know it.

855
01:00:00,705 --> 01:00:04,626
<i>Nil desperandum, dear lady. Across
the desert lies the Promised Land.</i>

856
01:00:04,751 --> 01:00:06,252
WONKA: Goodbye, Mrs. Gloop.

857
01:00:06,336 --> 01:00:10,507
<i>WONKA: Adieu. Auf Wiedersehen.
Gesundheit. Farewell.</i>

858
01:00:11,591 --> 01:00:14,677
<i>[Singing] Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do</i>

859
01:00:14,761 --> 01:00:18,014
<i>I've got a perfect puzzle for you</i>

860
01:00:18,097 --> 01:00:21,267
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompadee Dee</i>

861
01:00:21,351 --> 01:00:24,479
<i>If you are wise you'll listen to me</i>

862
01:00:24,687 --> 01:00:27,690
<i>What do you get
when you guzzle down sweets?</i>

863
01:00:28,024 --> 01:00:31,027
<i>Eating as much as an elephant eats?</i>

864
01:00:31,277 --> 01:00:34,280
<i>What are you at getting terribly fat?</i>

865
01:00:34,447 --> 01:00:38,409
<i>What do you think will come of that?</i>

866
01:00:39,369 --> 01:00:41,162
<i>I don't like the look of it</i>

867
01:00:41,246 --> 01:00:44,040
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompadee Da</i>

868
01:00:44,290 --> 01:00:47,377
<i>If you're not greedy, you will go far</i>

869
01:00:47,627 --> 01:00:50,547
<i>You will live in happiness, too</i>

870
01:00:50,964 --> 01:00:54,759
<i>Like the Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do</i>

871
01:01:04,477 --> 01:01:07,647
What kind of place
are you running here, anyhow, Wonka?

872
01:01:07,814 --> 01:01:09,232
[Speaking in French]

873
01:01:11,401 --> 01:01:12,819
What's he talking about?

874
01:01:21,995 --> 01:01:24,789
[Soft instrumental music]

875
01:01:31,838 --> 01:01:33,256
Wow, what a boat!

876
01:01:33,339 --> 01:01:35,216
Looks good enough to eat.

877
01:01:35,300 --> 01:01:38,094
<i>That's quite a nice little canoe
you've got there, Wonka.</i>

878
01:01:38,178 --> 01:01:42,223
" All I ask is a tall ship
and a star to sail her by"

879
01:01:42,432 --> 01:01:45,852
WONKA: All aboard, everybody.
MR. SALT: Ladies first. That means Veruca.

880
01:01:45,935 --> 01:01:48,855
If she's a lady, I'm a Vermicious Knid.

881
01:01:48,938 --> 01:01:52,775
- You sure this thing will float, eh. Wonka ?
- With your buoyancy, sir. Rest assured.

882
01:01:52,859 --> 01:01:55,028
<i>She's très jolie, but is she seaworthy?</i>

883
01:01:55,111 --> 01:01:58,948
Nothing to worry about, my dear lady.
I take good care of my guests.

884
01:01:59,032 --> 01:02:02,952
- Yeah, you took real good care of that August kid
..over there, yeah sure.
- Everybody aboard.

885
01:02:04,370 --> 01:02:06,247
<i>You're going to love this.</i>

886
01:02:06,456 --> 01:02:07,790
<i>Just love it.</i>

887
01:02:08,416 --> 01:02:10,043
[Bell ringing]

888
01:02:13,755 --> 01:02:15,924
[Lilting instrumental music]

889
01:02:38,321 --> 01:02:42,283
Daddy, I want a boat like this.
A beautiful paddle boat, that's what i want.

890
01:02:42,367 --> 01:02:45,036
What she wants
is a good kick in the pants.

891
01:02:45,119 --> 01:02:46,704
I'm going to be seasick.

892
01:02:46,788 --> 01:02:48,498
WONKA: Here, try one of this.
MRS. TEEVEE: What are they?

893
01:02:48,581 --> 01:02:51,292
WONKA: Rainbow drops.
Suck 'em, you can spit in seven colors.

894
01:02:51,376 --> 01:02:52,877
Spitting's a dirty habit.

895
01:02:52,961 --> 01:02:54,546
I know a worse one.

896
01:02:55,004 --> 01:02:57,674
[Lilting instrumental music continues]

897
01:03:06,432 --> 01:03:09,269
- What business are you in, Salt?
- Nuts.

898
01:03:19,946 --> 01:03:21,364
[Tense instrumental music]

899
01:03:21,447 --> 01:03:22,615
Here.

900
01:03:22,698 --> 01:03:26,369
A small step for mankind,
but a giant step for us.

901
01:03:26,702 --> 01:03:30,248
- All ashore.
- Let me off this freight.

902
01:03:30,331 --> 01:03:33,709
MIKE: Why don't they show stuff like that
on TV?

903
01:03:33,793 --> 01:03:37,505
- What a nightmare.
- Daddy, I do not want a boat like this.

904
01:03:37,672 --> 01:03:38,631
CHARLIE: " Dairy Cream. "

905
01:03:42,718 --> 01:03:44,345
BOTH: " Hair cream"?

906
01:03:44,554 --> 01:03:47,473
[Wonka speaking in German]

907
01:03:47,765 --> 01:03:49,225
That's not French.

908
01:03:49,475 --> 01:03:51,853
[Continues speaking in German]

909
01:03:55,731 --> 01:03:57,692
I can't take much more of this.

910
01:03:59,735 --> 01:04:01,612
<i>Der Inventing Room.</i>

911
01:04:02,947 --> 01:04:05,491
Now remember, no messing about.

912
01:04:05,575 --> 01:04:08,411
No touching, no tasting, no telling.

913
01:04:08,494 --> 01:04:09,579
GRANDPA: No telling what?

914
01:04:09,662 --> 01:04:13,875
All of my most secret inventions
are cooking and simmering in here.

915
01:04:13,958 --> 01:04:17,879
<i>Old Slugworth would give his false teeth
to get inside for five minutes.</i>

916
01:04:17,962 --> 01:04:20,506
So don't touch a thing.

917
01:04:28,055 --> 01:04:29,932
[Electronic beeping]

918
01:04:46,365 --> 01:04:49,410
Inventing Room?
Looks more like a Turkish bath to me.

919
01:04:49,494 --> 01:04:52,497
Even if Slugworth did get in here,
he couldn't find anything.

920
01:04:52,580 --> 01:04:55,750
- You got a garbage strike going on?
- Who does your cleaning up?

921
01:04:55,833 --> 01:04:59,754
<i>Shouldn't you wear rubber gloves?
You'll have the health inspectors after you.</i>

922
01:04:59,837 --> 01:05:03,633
Invention, my dear friends,
is 93% perspiration...

923
01:05:03,758 --> 01:05:05,927
6% electricity...

924
01:05:06,010 --> 01:05:07,929
4% evaporation...

925
01:05:08,012 --> 01:05:10,431
and 2% butterscotch ripple.

926
01:05:10,515 --> 01:05:12,725
MRS. TEEVEE: That's 105%.

927
01:05:12,934 --> 01:05:14,185
MR. SALT: Any good?

928
01:05:15,561 --> 01:05:16,729
[Ln high-pitched voice] Yes.

929
01:05:17,104 --> 01:05:18,523
Excuse me.

930
01:05:20,942 --> 01:05:24,362
Time is a precious thing. Never waste it.

931
01:05:26,197 --> 01:05:29,951
- He's absolutely bonkers.
- That's not bad.

932
01:05:32,453 --> 01:05:35,915
WONKA: " In spring time
The only pretty ring time

933
01:05:35,998 --> 01:05:39,127
" Birds sing, hey ding a ding, ding

934
01:05:39,210 --> 01:05:42,755
<i>" Sweet lovers love the spring"</i>

935
01:05:42,839 --> 01:05:44,215
[Explosion]

936
01:05:46,175 --> 01:05:48,386
I told you not to, silly boy.

937
01:05:48,469 --> 01:05:49,887
Your teeth!

938
01:05:49,971 --> 01:05:51,514
Boy, that's great stuff.

939
01:05:51,931 --> 01:05:55,560
That's exploding candy for your enemies.
Great idea, isn't it?

940
01:05:55,643 --> 01:05:58,646
Not ready yet, though. Still too weak.
Needs more gelignite.

941
01:06:08,406 --> 01:06:09,782
What's that for?

942
01:06:09,907 --> 01:06:11,617
Gives it a little kick.

943
01:06:17,248 --> 01:06:19,375
Butterscotch? Buttergin?

944
01:06:19,500 --> 01:06:21,669
<i>Got something going on inside, have you?</i>

945
01:06:21,752 --> 01:06:24,672
WONKA: " Candy is dandy
but liquor is quicker. "

946
01:06:28,342 --> 01:06:30,845
MIKE: What's the matter?
Too hot, Mr. Wonka?

947
01:06:30,928 --> 01:06:33,097
Too cold. Far too cold.

948
01:06:33,181 --> 01:06:35,516
MR. SALT: That's gourmet cooking for you.

949
01:06:36,350 --> 01:06:38,394
No! Don't, please.

950
01:06:38,728 --> 01:06:41,230
<i>Forgive me,
but no one must look under there.</i>

951
01:06:41,314 --> 01:06:44,817
This is the most secret machine
in my entire factory.

952
01:06:45,026 --> 01:06:48,946
WONKA: This will sizzle old Slugworth.
CHARLIE: What does it do?

953
01:06:50,114 --> 01:06:52,283
Would you like to see?

954
01:06:54,660 --> 01:06:56,537
[Buzzing]

955
01:07:15,556 --> 01:07:17,058
CHARLIE: But what does it do?

956
01:07:17,141 --> 01:07:19,894
Can't you see?
It makes Everlasting Gobstoppers.

957
01:07:19,977 --> 01:07:22,063
VIOLET: Did you say
Everlasting Gobstoppers?

958
01:07:22,146 --> 01:07:25,566
For children with very little pocket money.
You can suck them forever.

959
01:07:25,650 --> 01:07:27,360
I want an Everlasting Gobstopper.

960
01:07:27,443 --> 01:07:28,820
- Me, too.
- And me.

961
01:07:28,903 --> 01:07:31,405
Fantastic invention.
Revolutionize the industry.

962
01:07:31,489 --> 01:07:35,243
You can suck them and suck them
and they'll never get any smaller. Never.

963
01:07:35,326 --> 01:07:36,577
At least, I don't think so.

964
01:07:36,661 --> 01:07:38,830
WONKA: Few more tests.
MIKE: How do you make them?

965
01:07:38,913 --> 01:07:42,583
I'm a trifle deaf in this ear.
Speak a little louder next time.

966
01:07:42,667 --> 01:07:44,335
WONKA: Who wants
an Everlasting Gobstopper?

967
01:07:44,418 --> 01:07:46,087
CHILDREN: I do.

968
01:07:47,088 --> 01:07:51,467
<i>I can only give them to you
if you swear to keep them for yourselves...</i>

969
01:07:51,676 --> 01:07:56,055
and never show them to another
living soul as long as you all shall live.

970
01:07:56,264 --> 01:07:58,141
WONKA: Agreed?
CHILDREN: Agreed.

971
01:07:58,349 --> 01:08:01,978
Good. One for you,
and one for you, and one for you.

972
01:08:02,061 --> 01:08:03,563
What about Charlie?

973
01:08:03,688 --> 01:08:05,106
WONKA: And one for Charlie.

974
01:08:05,189 --> 01:08:07,692
She's got two. I want another one.

975
01:08:07,775 --> 01:08:10,695
VIOLET: Stop squawking, you twit!
WONKA: Everybody has had one.

976
01:08:10,778 --> 01:08:14,031
And one is enough for anybody.
Now come along.

977
01:08:15,741 --> 01:08:17,368
WONKA: Over here, if you'll follow me...

978
01:08:17,785 --> 01:08:19,954
<i>I have something rather special
to show you.</i>

979
01:08:20,037 --> 01:08:21,747
MR. SALT: It's special, all right.

980
01:08:22,039 --> 01:08:24,375
I only hope my Veruca doesn't want one.

981
01:08:24,459 --> 01:08:26,961
MIKE: What a contraption.
WONKA: Isn't she scrumptious?

982
01:08:27,044 --> 01:08:30,423
<i>She's my revolutionary,
non-pollutionary mechanical wonder.</i>

983
01:08:30,840 --> 01:08:33,384
Button, button, who's got the button?

984
01:08:33,801 --> 01:08:35,303
CHARLIE: It's over there.

985
01:08:35,887 --> 01:08:37,054
WONKA: Here?
CHARLIE: Yeah.

986
01:08:37,513 --> 01:08:39,765
[Whizzing and whirring]

987
01:08:53,154 --> 01:08:55,406
WONKA: What you are witnessing,
dear friends...

988
01:08:55,490 --> 01:08:58,284
is the most enormous miracle
of the Machine Age.

989
01:08:58,367 --> 01:09:01,287
<i>The creation of a confectionery giant.</i>

990
01:09:02,622 --> 01:09:05,208
[Whizzing and whirring continues]

991
01:09:11,255 --> 01:09:13,424
<i>WONKA: Finito.
VERUCA: That's all?</i>

992
01:09:13,508 --> 01:09:15,510
Don't you know what this is?

993
01:09:15,593 --> 01:09:17,470
VIOLET: By gum, it's gum!
WONKA: Wrong.

994
01:09:17,553 --> 01:09:21,390
It's the most amazing, fabulous,
sensational gum in the whole world.

995
01:09:21,474 --> 01:09:22,767
What's so fab about it?

996
01:09:22,850 --> 01:09:26,103
- This tiny gum is a three-course dinner.
- Bull.

997
01:09:26,187 --> 01:09:29,357
WONKA: Roast beef, it's not quite right yet.
VIOLET: I don't care.

998
01:09:29,440 --> 01:09:31,526
I wouldn't do that. I really wouldn't.

999
01:09:31,609 --> 01:09:33,444
So long as it's gum, that's for me.

1000
01:09:33,528 --> 01:09:35,780
Violet, don't do anything stupid.

1001
01:09:35,863 --> 01:09:37,865
What's it taste like?

1002
01:09:37,949 --> 01:09:40,785
Madness! It's tomato soup.

1003
01:09:40,952 --> 01:09:44,038
It's hot and creamy.
I can feel it running down my throat.

1004
01:09:44,122 --> 01:09:45,581
Stop. Don't.

1005
01:09:45,665 --> 01:09:49,836
- Why doesn't she listen to Mr. Wonka?
- Because, Charlie, she's a nitwit.

1006
01:09:49,919 --> 01:09:51,671
This sure is great soup.

1007
01:09:51,796 --> 01:09:53,714
The second course is coming up.

1008
01:09:53,798 --> 01:09:56,134
Roast beef and a baked potato.

1009
01:09:56,676 --> 01:09:58,427
With sour cream?

1010
01:09:59,637 --> 01:10:02,807
SAM: What's for dessert, baby?
VIOLET: Dessert? Here it comes.

1011
01:10:02,890 --> 01:10:06,894
<i>Blueberry pie and cream.
It's the most marvelous pie I've tasted.</i>

1012
01:10:06,978 --> 01:10:09,272
SAM: Holy Toledo!
What's happening to your face?

1013
01:10:09,355 --> 01:10:10,982
Cool it, Dad. Let me finish.

1014
01:10:11,065 --> 01:10:12,817
But your face is turning blue.

1015
01:10:12,900 --> 01:10:16,279
SAM: You're turning violet, Violet!
VIOLET: What are you talking about?

1016
01:10:16,362 --> 01:10:18,448
I told you I hadn't got it right yet.

1017
01:10:18,531 --> 01:10:21,159
You can say that again.
Look what it's done to my kid.

1018
01:10:21,242 --> 01:10:24,996
It always goes wrong
when we come to the dessert. Always.

1019
01:10:25,121 --> 01:10:27,081
Violet, what are you doing now?

1020
01:10:27,165 --> 01:10:29,083
- You're blowing up!
- I feel funny!

1021
01:10:29,167 --> 01:10:31,419
GRANDPA: I'm not surprised.
VIOLET: What's happening?

1022
01:10:31,502 --> 01:10:33,504
SAM: You're blowing up!
WONKA: Like a blueberry.

1023
01:10:33,588 --> 01:10:35,923
SAM: Call a doctor!
MRS. TEEVEE: Stick her with a pin.

1024
01:10:36,007 --> 01:10:38,509
CHARLIE: She'll pop.
WONKA: It happens every time.

1025
01:10:38,593 --> 01:10:41,762
<i>- They all become blueberries.
- You've really done it this time!</i>

1026
01:10:41,846 --> 01:10:43,514
I'll break you for this.

1027
01:10:43,598 --> 01:10:46,809
- Well, I'll get it right in the end.
- Help!

1028
01:10:47,602 --> 01:10:50,771
SAM: Let the air out of her quick.
WONKA: There's no air.

1029
01:10:50,855 --> 01:10:52,607
- That's juice.
- Juice?

1030
01:10:52,690 --> 01:10:55,902
Would you roll the young lady
down to the juicing room at once?

1031
01:10:55,985 --> 01:10:58,029
SAM: What for?
WONKA: For squeezing.

1032
01:10:58,112 --> 01:11:00,698
She has to be squeezed immediately
before she explodes.

1033
01:11:00,782 --> 01:11:03,367
- Explodes?
- It's a fairly simple operation.

1034
01:11:03,451 --> 01:11:07,205
<i>[Singing] Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do</i>

1035
01:11:07,288 --> 01:11:10,750
<i>I've got another puzzle for you</i>

1036
01:11:10,958 --> 01:11:14,128
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompada Dee</i>

1037
01:11:14,712 --> 01:11:18,174
<i>If you are wise you'll listen to me</i>

1038
01:11:18,424 --> 01:11:21,636
<i>Gum chewing's fine
When it's once in a while</i>

1039
01:11:21,719 --> 01:11:25,556
<i>It stops you from smoking
And brightens your smile</i>

1040
01:11:25,640 --> 01:11:28,935
<i>But it's repulsive, revolting, and wrong</i>

1041
01:11:29,060 --> 01:11:33,231
<i>Chewing and chewing all day long</i>

1042
01:11:34,732 --> 01:11:36,734
<i>The way that a cow does</i>

1043
01:11:38,319 --> 01:11:41,906
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompadee Da</i>

1044
01:11:41,989 --> 01:11:45,201
<i>Given good manners, you will go far</i>

1045
01:11:45,660 --> 01:11:48,830
<i>You will live in happiness, too</i>

1046
01:11:49,163 --> 01:11:53,417
<i>Like the Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do</i>

1047
01:11:57,672 --> 01:12:01,092
I'll get even with you for this,
if it's the last thing I ever do!

1048
01:12:01,175 --> 01:12:03,761
I got a blueberry for a daughter.

1049
01:12:04,720 --> 01:12:08,516
" Where is fancy bred,
In the heart or in the head?"

1050
01:12:09,976 --> 01:12:11,519
Shall we roll on?

1051
01:12:16,065 --> 01:12:17,400
WONKA: Thank you.

1052
01:12:17,942 --> 01:12:22,530
Well, well, two naughty,
nasty little children gone.

1053
01:12:22,947 --> 01:12:26,367
Three good, sweet little children left.

1054
01:12:28,453 --> 01:12:31,205
WONKA: Hurry, please. Long way to go yet.

1055
01:12:34,792 --> 01:12:37,003
Wait a minute. I must show you this.

1056
01:12:37,128 --> 01:12:39,881
Lickable wallpaper for nursery walls.

1057
01:12:39,964 --> 01:12:41,841
Lick an orange, it tastes like an orange.

1058
01:12:41,924 --> 01:12:45,761
Lick a pineapple, it tastes like a pineapple.
Go ahead, try it.

1059
01:12:46,179 --> 01:12:48,055
MRS. TEEVEE: I got a plum.

1060
01:12:48,139 --> 01:12:51,809
CHARLIE: Grandpa, this banana's fantastic.
It tastes so real.

1061
01:12:52,018 --> 01:12:55,229
Try some more.
The strawberries taste like strawberries.

1062
01:12:55,313 --> 01:12:57,815
The snozzberries taste like snozzberries.

1063
01:12:57,899 --> 01:13:00,777
Snozzberries?
Whoever heard of a snozzberry?

1064
01:13:02,111 --> 01:13:04,530
" We are the music makers.

1065
01:13:04,864 --> 01:13:07,825
" And we are the dreamers of dreams. "

1066
01:13:09,285 --> 01:13:10,787
Come along.

1067
01:13:13,790 --> 01:13:16,375
Something very unusual in here.

1068
01:13:16,876 --> 01:13:20,171
WONKA: Bubbles, bubbles everywhere,
but not a drop to drink.

1069
01:13:20,254 --> 01:13:23,049
WONKA: Yet.
CHARLIE: What's it making, Mr. Wonka?

1070
01:13:23,132 --> 01:13:25,551
WONKA: Fizzy-Lifting drinks.
They fill you with gas...

1071
01:13:25,635 --> 01:13:29,764
and the gas is so lifting, that it lifts you
right off the ground like a balloon.

1072
01:13:29,847 --> 01:13:32,183
VERUCA: Isn't it high? Gosh!

1073
01:13:32,350 --> 01:13:36,395
- But I dare not sell it yet. It's too powerful.
- Let us try some, please.

1074
01:13:36,479 --> 01:13:40,858
No, absolutely not. There'd be children
floating around all over the place.

1075
01:13:41,067 --> 01:13:43,319
Come along, don't hang about.

1076
01:13:43,653 --> 01:13:46,072
<i>You'll be wild about this next room.</i>

1077
01:13:47,156 --> 01:13:49,826
Let's take a drink. Nobody's watching.

1078
01:13:49,909 --> 01:13:51,661
A small one won't hurt us.

1079
01:13:57,667 --> 01:13:59,001
Not bad.

1080
01:14:00,670 --> 01:14:01,921
Well?

1081
01:14:02,171 --> 01:14:03,923
Nothing's happening.

1082
01:14:04,006 --> 01:14:06,717
GRANDPA: You're right, Charlie.
I can't understand why.

1083
01:14:06,801 --> 01:14:08,177
[Grandpa shrieks]

1084
01:14:09,679 --> 01:14:11,389
I feel terribly strange.

1085
01:14:11,514 --> 01:14:14,350
- What do we do now?
- I don't know, Charlie.

1086
01:14:14,767 --> 01:14:17,562
We're in big trouble.
Mr. Wonka isn't going to like this.

1087
01:14:17,645 --> 01:14:19,355
We can't stay up here all day.

1088
01:14:19,438 --> 01:14:21,482
- You're right but-
- I'll try and get down.

1089
01:14:21,566 --> 01:14:24,569
All right, Charlie,
but please be very careful.

1090
01:14:25,778 --> 01:14:29,532
It's fun, Grandpa. It works.
Come on in, the air's fine.

1091
01:14:29,615 --> 01:14:32,702
<i>- I haven't been swimming in 20 years.
- Give me your hand.</i>

1092
01:14:32,785 --> 01:14:34,704
I don't think I ought to.

1093
01:14:34,829 --> 01:14:37,123
[Light instrumental music]

1094
01:14:37,373 --> 01:14:39,292
This is great!

1095
01:14:39,709 --> 01:14:43,171
CHARLIE: Try this, Grandpa.
GRANDPA: All right, Charlie. Wait for me.

1096
01:14:43,629 --> 01:14:47,091
[Grandpa and Charlie squeal with delight]

1097
01:14:47,550 --> 01:14:50,428
GRANDPA: I'm a shooting star.
CHARLIE: I'm a rocket!

1098
01:14:51,888 --> 01:14:53,764
This is really great.

1099
01:14:53,890 --> 01:14:56,184
Look, I'm a bird!

1100
01:14:58,060 --> 01:15:00,229
GRANDPA: I feel light as a feather.

1101
01:15:01,063 --> 01:15:03,983
Look down. We're really high now.

1102
01:15:08,488 --> 01:15:10,156
Watch this, Grandpa.

1103
01:15:13,743 --> 01:15:15,244
Wonderful, Charlie.

1104
01:15:15,411 --> 01:15:17,747
- Try it, Grandpa.
- I don't know.

1105
01:15:17,830 --> 01:15:20,458
- Come on, Grandpa.
- All right.

1106
01:15:23,336 --> 01:15:25,296
You did it, Grandpa!

1107
01:15:25,755 --> 01:15:26,839
[Grandpa groans]

1108
01:15:26,923 --> 01:15:28,841
I think I hit an air pocket.

1109
01:15:28,925 --> 01:15:31,093
You can fly to the moon this way.

1110
01:15:31,177 --> 01:15:32,929
GRANDPA: Let's just fly south
for the winter.

1111
01:15:33,012 --> 01:15:34,305
CHARLIE: Why not?

1112
01:15:36,516 --> 01:15:39,268
- I'm a bird!
- I'm a plane!

1113
01:15:39,352 --> 01:15:40,603
I'm...

1114
01:15:40,686 --> 01:15:42,522
going too high!

1115
01:15:43,356 --> 01:15:45,608
Grandpa, I can't get down!

1116
01:15:45,733 --> 01:15:48,611
Help! Grandpa, the fan!

1117
01:15:48,694 --> 01:15:51,030
GRANDPA: Stay away from it!
It'll chop us to bits!

1118
01:15:51,114 --> 01:15:55,034
GRANDPA: We're in trouble! I can't stop!
CHARLIE: It's pulling me in!

1119
01:15:55,118 --> 01:15:56,994
GRANDPA: I can't stop!

1120
01:15:57,203 --> 01:16:00,123
CHARLIE: What do we do?
GRANDPA: Grab hold of something quick!

1121
01:16:00,206 --> 01:16:02,792
CHARLIE: There's nothing
to grab on to! Help!

1122
01:16:03,084 --> 01:16:06,546
CHARLIE: We're gonna get killed!
GRANDPA: Help!

1123
01:16:06,629 --> 01:16:09,465
Mr. Wonka, please! Turn off the fan!

1124
01:16:10,424 --> 01:16:11,467
[Burping]

1125
01:16:13,010 --> 01:16:15,888
I'm going down. Quick, Charlie, burp!

1126
01:16:15,972 --> 01:16:18,224
<i>If you don't, it'll cut you to ribbons!</i>

1127
01:16:18,307 --> 01:16:20,017
CHARLIE: I can't! Help!

1128
01:16:20,101 --> 01:16:21,894
<i>You've got to burp. It's the only way.</i>

1129
01:16:22,645 --> 01:16:24,772
<i>Attaboy. Burp again.</i>

1130
01:16:25,148 --> 01:16:27,024
<i>Attaboy. Come on.</i>

1131
01:16:28,067 --> 01:16:29,110
[Charlie burping]

1132
01:16:29,318 --> 01:16:31,487
That's wonderful, Charlie.

1133
01:16:37,994 --> 01:16:41,289
GRANDPA: Grab on to me.
We're going to be all right now.

1134
01:16:41,539 --> 01:16:43,416
[Both burping]

1135
01:16:45,418 --> 01:16:48,129
[Light instrumental music]

1136
01:16:49,213 --> 01:16:50,423
Good boy.

1137
01:16:51,007 --> 01:16:53,468
From now on,
we keep our feet on the ground.

1138
01:16:53,551 --> 01:16:55,678
Let's catch up to the others.

1139
01:16:58,347 --> 01:17:02,185
I know what you're thinking.
They can't be doing what they're doing.

1140
01:17:02,393 --> 01:17:04,395
<i>But they are. They have to.</i>

1141
01:17:04,479 --> 01:17:07,273
I haven't met the Oompa Loompa yet
who could do it.

1142
01:17:07,356 --> 01:17:09,692
WONKA: These are the geese
that lay the golden eggs.

1143
01:17:09,776 --> 01:17:11,903
WONKA: They're larger
than ordinary geese.

1144
01:17:11,986 --> 01:17:16,115
WONKA: In fact, they're quadruple-size
geese which produce octuple-size eggs.

1145
01:17:16,199 --> 01:17:18,201
WONKA: They're laying overtime for Easter.

1146
01:17:18,284 --> 01:17:20,036
But Easter's over.

1147
01:17:20,453 --> 01:17:24,373
[Whispering] They don't know that.
I'm trying to get ahead for next year.

1148
01:17:25,708 --> 01:17:28,294
MR. SALT: What happens
if they drop one of those eggs?

1149
01:17:28,377 --> 01:17:30,963
WONKA: An omelet fit for a king, sir.

1150
01:17:31,297 --> 01:17:33,007
VERUCA: Are they chocolate eggs?

1151
01:17:33,132 --> 01:17:35,927
Golden chocolate eggs.
That's a great delicacy.

1152
01:17:36,135 --> 01:17:37,720
But don't get too close.

1153
01:17:37,804 --> 01:17:40,556
<i>The geese are temperamental.
So we have the Eggdicator.</i>

1154
01:17:40,640 --> 01:17:42,725
MRS. TEEVEE: Egg-di-what?
WONKA: The Eggdicator.

1155
01:17:42,809 --> 01:17:45,353
<i>It can tell the difference
between a good egg...</i>

1156
01:17:45,436 --> 01:17:46,687
and a bad egg.

1157
01:17:46,771 --> 01:17:50,566
<i>If it's a good egg, it's shined up
and shipped out all over the world.</i>

1158
01:17:50,733 --> 01:17:52,652
But if it's a bad egg...

1159
01:17:53,569 --> 01:17:55,071
down the chute.

1160
01:17:56,739 --> 01:17:58,116
[Horn honking]

1161
01:17:59,325 --> 01:18:02,787
- It's an educated Eggdicator.
- It's a lot of nonsense.

1162
01:18:03,329 --> 01:18:06,415
<i>[Singing] A little nonsense now and then</i>

1163
01:18:06,499 --> 01:18:09,836
<i>Is relished by the wisest men</i>

1164
01:18:10,336 --> 01:18:12,255
VERUCA: Daddy, I want a golden goose.

1165
01:18:12,505 --> 01:18:15,466
CHARLIE: Here we go again.
MR. SALT: All right, sweetheart.

1166
01:18:15,675 --> 01:18:18,010
You'll get one as soon as we get home.

1167
01:18:18,094 --> 01:18:20,429
No, I want one of those.

1168
01:18:21,097 --> 01:18:23,099
How much do you want for the goose?

1169
01:18:23,182 --> 01:18:25,518
WONKA: They're not for sale.
MR. SALT: Name your price.

1170
01:18:25,601 --> 01:18:27,770
<i>WONKA: She can't have one.
VERUCA: Who says so?</i>

1171
01:18:27,854 --> 01:18:31,524
- The man with the funny hat.
- I want one. I want a golden goose.

1172
01:18:32,024 --> 01:18:34,485
Gooses. Geeses.

1173
01:18:34,694 --> 01:18:37,447
I want my geese
to lay golden eggs for Easter.

1174
01:18:37,530 --> 01:18:39,866
MR. SALT: It will, dear.
VERUCA: At least 100 a day.

1175
01:18:39,949 --> 01:18:42,952
MR. SALT: Anything you say.
VERUCA: And by the way...

1176
01:18:43,119 --> 01:18:46,456
VERUCA: I want a feast.
MR. SALT: You ate before you came here.

1177
01:18:46,539 --> 01:18:48,291
<i>[Singing] I want a bean-feast</i>

1178
01:18:48,374 --> 01:18:49,459
One of those.

1179
01:18:49,542 --> 01:18:52,545
<i>[Singing] Cream buns and doughnuts
And fruitcake with no nuts</i>

1180
01:18:52,628 --> 01:18:54,380
<i>So good you could go nuts</i>

1181
01:18:54,464 --> 01:18:58,968
<i>- You'll have them when you get home.
- No, now. I want a ball.</i>

1182
01:18:59,552 --> 01:19:01,637
<i>[Singing] I want a party</i>

1183
01:19:01,888 --> 01:19:04,182
<i>Pink macaroons and a million balloons</i>

1184
01:19:04,265 --> 01:19:06,476
<i>And performing baboons and...</i>

1185
01:19:06,559 --> 01:19:07,852
<i>Give it to me</i>

1186
01:19:08,269 --> 01:19:09,395
Now.

1187
01:19:09,479 --> 01:19:11,647
<i>[Singing] I want the world</i>

1188
01:19:11,814 --> 01:19:14,150
<i>I want the whole world</i>

1189
01:19:14,317 --> 01:19:18,654
<i>I want to lock it all up in my pocket
It's my bar of chocolate</i>

1190
01:19:18,821 --> 01:19:21,824
<i>Give it to me now</i>

1191
01:19:21,991 --> 01:19:23,826
<i>I want today</i>

1192
01:19:24,327 --> 01:19:26,370
<i>I want tomorrow</i>

1193
01:19:26,579 --> 01:19:29,207
<i>I want to wear them
Like braids in my hair</i>

1194
01:19:29,415 --> 01:19:31,501
<i>And I don't want to share them!</i>

1195
01:19:35,338 --> 01:19:39,008
<i>I want a party with roomfuls of laughter</i>

1196
01:19:39,175 --> 01:19:42,011
<i>Ten thousand tons of ice cream</i>

1197
01:19:42,178 --> 01:19:45,890
<i>And if I don't get the things I am after</i>

1198
01:19:46,766 --> 01:19:50,728
<i>I'm going to scream</i>

1199
01:20:00,613 --> 01:20:02,031
<i>I want the works</i>

1200
01:20:02,115 --> 01:20:03,616
<i>I want the whole works</i>

1201
01:20:03,699 --> 01:20:06,369
<i>Presents and prizes
And sweets and surprises</i>

1202
01:20:06,536 --> 01:20:09,330
<i>Of all shapes and sizes
And now</i>

1203
01:20:09,455 --> 01:20:13,459
<i>Don't care how
I want it now</i>

1204
01:20:18,172 --> 01:20:19,507
[Horn honking]

1205
01:20:26,055 --> 01:20:27,807
She was a bad egg.

1206
01:20:29,183 --> 01:20:30,643
Where's she gone?

1207
01:20:30,726 --> 01:20:33,896
Where all the other bad eggs go.
Down the garbage chute.

1208
01:20:34,147 --> 01:20:36,149
The garbage chute?

1209
01:20:36,732 --> 01:20:39,527
- Where does it lead to?
- To the furnace.

1210
01:20:40,236 --> 01:20:42,071
The furnace?

1211
01:20:42,738 --> 01:20:45,825
- She'll be sizzled like a sausage.
- Not necessarily.

1212
01:20:45,908 --> 01:20:48,369
She could be stuck inside the tube.

1213
01:20:48,619 --> 01:20:49,745
Hold on!

1214
01:20:49,829 --> 01:20:52,915
MR. SALT: Veruca, sweetheart.
Daddy's coming!

1215
01:20:56,002 --> 01:20:58,171
There's gonna be a lot of garbage today.

1216
01:20:58,254 --> 01:21:01,132
Mr. Salt finally got what he wanted.

1217
01:21:01,340 --> 01:21:03,926
- What's that?
- Veruca went first.

1218
01:21:04,093 --> 01:21:07,972
CHARLIE: Mr. Wonka, they won't really
be burned in the furnace, will they?

1219
01:21:08,848 --> 01:21:11,767
I think that furnace is lit
only every other day.

1220
01:21:11,934 --> 01:21:14,520
<i>So they have a good sporting chance.</i>

1221
01:21:15,271 --> 01:21:18,775
<i>[Singing] Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do</i>

1222
01:21:18,941 --> 01:21:22,445
<i>I've got another puzzle for you</i>

1223
01:21:22,612 --> 01:21:25,865
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompadee Dee</i>

1224
01:21:26,199 --> 01:21:29,619
<i>If you are wise you'll listen to me</i>

1225
01:21:29,869 --> 01:21:33,331
<i>Who do you blame when your kid is a brat</i>

1226
01:21:33,414 --> 01:21:36,793
<i>Pampered and spoiled like a Siamese cat?</i>

1227
01:21:36,876 --> 01:21:40,213
<i>Blaming the kids is a lie and a shame</i>

1228
01:21:40,296 --> 01:21:44,175
<i>You know exactly who's to blame</i>

1229
01:21:45,134 --> 01:21:47,637
<i>The mother and the father</i>

1230
01:21:48,971 --> 01:21:52,183
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompadee Da</i>

1231
01:21:52,475 --> 01:21:55,978
<i>If you're not spoiled then you will go far</i>

1232
01:21:56,145 --> 01:22:00,108
<i>You will live in happiness, too</i>

1233
01:22:01,400 --> 01:22:05,571
<i>Like the Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do</i>

1234
01:22:06,781 --> 01:22:09,742
WONKA: I can't understand.
The children are disappearing like rabbits.

1235
01:22:09,826 --> 01:22:13,079
<i>Well, we still have each other.
Shall we press on?</i>

1236
01:22:13,162 --> 01:22:15,248
Can't we sit down for a minute?

1237
01:22:15,331 --> 01:22:19,502
- The pace is killing me.
- Transportation has been arranged.

1238
01:22:24,173 --> 01:22:26,259
WONKA: Behold the Wonkamobile.

1239
01:22:26,342 --> 01:22:28,594
" A thing of beauty is a joy forever. "

1240
01:22:28,678 --> 01:22:31,514
WONKA: Places, please.
The dance is about to begin.

1241
01:22:31,639 --> 01:22:34,267
WONKA: Better grab a seat,
they're going fast.

1242
01:22:34,517 --> 01:22:37,019
GRANDPA: What's that
they're filling it up with?

1243
01:22:37,186 --> 01:22:40,106
Ginger ale, ginger pop,
ginger beer, beer bubbles...

1244
01:22:40,189 --> 01:22:43,609
bubbleade, bubble cola, double cola,
double-bubble burp-a-cola...

1245
01:22:43,693 --> 01:22:46,696
and all that crazy carbonated stuff
that tickles your nose.

1246
01:22:46,863 --> 01:22:49,949
Few people realize
the tremendous power in those things.

1247
01:22:50,032 --> 01:22:51,909
I'm sorry I asked.

1248
01:22:51,993 --> 01:22:55,288
[Whispering] You think Slugworth would
pay extra to know about this?

1249
01:22:55,371 --> 01:22:58,332
Just keep your eyes open
and your mouth shut.

1250
01:22:59,834 --> 01:23:02,628
WONKA: Everybody set?
CHARLIE: Is this gonna go fast?

1251
01:23:02,712 --> 01:23:05,465
It should. It's got more gas in it
than a politician.

1252
01:23:05,548 --> 01:23:09,510
Hold on tight. I'm going to open her up
and see what she can do.

1253
01:23:17,727 --> 01:23:19,729
WONKA: " Swifter than eagles...

1254
01:23:20,021 --> 01:23:22,064
" stronger than lions! "

1255
01:23:22,440 --> 01:23:23,941
[Screaming]

1256
01:23:30,031 --> 01:23:31,741
MIKE: It's getting in my eyes!

1257
01:23:32,366 --> 01:23:34,243
MRS. TEEVEE: It's seeping in my shoes!

1258
01:23:34,410 --> 01:23:37,079
MRS. TEEVEE: I'm soaked!
It'll never come out!

1259
01:23:37,371 --> 01:23:39,081
MIKE: It's sticking to my gun.

1260
01:23:40,917 --> 01:23:42,502
[Wonka singing]

1261
01:23:47,423 --> 01:23:50,635
My dress! My hair! My face!

1262
01:23:51,928 --> 01:23:54,764
MRS. TEEVEE: I'm sending you
the cleaning bill, Mr. Wonka.

1263
01:23:57,892 --> 01:23:59,769
MRS. TEEVEE: I'm dry cleaned.

1264
01:24:05,066 --> 01:24:06,651
Grandpa, what was that
we just went through?

1265
01:24:07,527 --> 01:24:08,820
Hsaw Aknow.

1266
01:24:08,903 --> 01:24:09,987
MRS. TEEVEE: Is that Japanese?

1267
01:24:10,071 --> 01:24:12,198
No, " Wonka Wash" spelled backwards.

1268
01:24:12,281 --> 01:24:14,534
WONKA: That's it. The journey's over.

1269
01:24:14,617 --> 01:24:18,579
<i>GRANDPA: Finest bath I've had in 20 years.
CHARLIE: Let's do it again, Mr. Wonka.</i>

1270
01:24:18,704 --> 01:24:22,041
<i>- That's as far as it goes?
- Couldn't we have walked?</i>

1271
01:24:22,542 --> 01:24:26,879
<i>If the good Lord had intended us to walk,
he wouldn't have invented roller skates.</i>

1272
01:24:26,963 --> 01:24:31,217
<i>Would you all please put these on?
We have to be very careful.</i>

1273
01:24:31,717 --> 01:24:34,053
There's dangerous stuff inside.

1274
01:24:36,681 --> 01:24:40,309
WONKA: Wonkavision. My very latest
and greatest invention.

1275
01:24:41,227 --> 01:24:43,563
MIKE: It's television.
WONKA: It's Wonkavision.

1276
01:24:43,646 --> 01:24:46,816
<i>I suppose you all know
how ordinary television works.</i>

1277
01:24:46,899 --> 01:24:49,485
- You photograph-
- I do. You photograph something...

1278
01:24:49,569 --> 01:24:52,196
then it's split into millions of pieces...

1279
01:24:52,280 --> 01:24:56,826
that go whizzing through the air
and down to your TV set in the right order.

1280
01:24:57,160 --> 01:25:00,413
You should open your mouth a little wider
when you speak.

1281
01:25:00,496 --> 01:25:03,040
So I thought,
" If they can do it with a photograph...

1282
01:25:03,124 --> 01:25:05,960
" why can't I do it with a bar of chocolate?"

1283
01:25:08,337 --> 01:25:12,633
WONKA: I shall now send this chocolate
bar from one end of the room to the other.

1284
01:25:12,717 --> 01:25:16,345
It has to be big, because when
you transmit something by television...

1285
01:25:16,429 --> 01:25:18,931
it always ends up smaller
on the other end.

1286
01:25:19,015 --> 01:25:20,683
Goggles on, please.

1287
01:25:22,727 --> 01:25:25,980
WONKA: Lights, camera, action!

1288
01:25:26,606 --> 01:25:27,857
[Screaming]

1289
01:25:28,107 --> 01:25:30,026
<i>You can remove your goggles.</i>

1290
01:25:30,359 --> 01:25:32,028
Where's the chocolate?

1291
01:25:32,111 --> 01:25:34,864
It's flying over our heads
in a million pieces.

1292
01:25:34,989 --> 01:25:36,574
[Electronic beeping]

1293
01:25:37,074 --> 01:25:38,743
WONKA: Now watch the screen.

1294
01:25:41,704 --> 01:25:43,039
WONKA: Here it comes.

1295
01:25:44,290 --> 01:25:45,750
WONKA: There it is.

1296
01:25:45,917 --> 01:25:49,003
- Take it.
- How can you take it? It's just a picture.

1297
01:25:49,086 --> 01:25:50,797
All right, you take it.

1298
01:25:51,964 --> 01:25:55,676
- It's real.
- Taste it. It's delicious. It's just smaller.

1299
01:25:58,387 --> 01:26:00,640
- It's perfect.
- It's unbelievable.

1300
01:26:00,723 --> 01:26:02,517
- It's a miracle.
- It's a TV dinner.

1301
01:26:02,600 --> 01:26:05,311
- It's Wonkavision.
- It could change the world.

1302
01:26:05,394 --> 01:26:07,146
Can you send other things?

1303
01:26:07,230 --> 01:26:09,565
MIKE: Not just chocolate.
WONKA: Anything you like.

1304
01:26:09,649 --> 01:26:12,068
- What about people?
- People?

1305
01:26:14,070 --> 01:26:16,781
I don't really know. I suppose I could.

1306
01:26:16,989 --> 01:26:19,992
Yes, I'm sure I could.
I'm pretty sure I could.

1307
01:26:20,076 --> 01:26:21,994
<i>But it might have some messy results.</i>

1308
01:26:22,078 --> 01:26:25,414
MIKE: Look! I'm going to be
the first person to be sent by television.

1309
01:26:25,498 --> 01:26:28,334
MRS. TEEVEE: Get away from that thing!
WONKA: Come back.

1310
01:26:28,417 --> 01:26:30,503
MIKE: Lights, camera, action!

1311
01:26:32,296 --> 01:26:33,798
[Electronic beeping]

1312
01:26:34,173 --> 01:26:35,550
Where are you?

1313
01:26:35,758 --> 01:26:37,885
GRANDPA: He's up there,
in a million pieces.

1314
01:26:37,969 --> 01:26:39,887
MRS. TEEVEE: Mike, are you there?

1315
01:26:39,971 --> 01:26:41,889
WONKA: No good shouting.
Watch the screen.

1316
01:26:41,973 --> 01:26:43,891
[Electronic beeping continues]

1317
01:26:44,851 --> 01:26:47,186
Mike? Why is he taking so long?

1318
01:26:47,270 --> 01:26:49,605
A million pieces take a long time
to put together.

1319
01:26:49,689 --> 01:26:52,525
MRS. TEEVEE: Where are they?
WONKA: There's something coming.

1320
01:26:52,608 --> 01:26:55,361
MRS. TEEVEE: Is it Mike?
WONKA: It's hard to tell but-

1321
01:26:55,862 --> 01:26:58,448
The little groover's getting smaller
by the minute.

1322
01:26:58,531 --> 01:27:02,743
MIKE: Look at me. I'm the first person
in the world to be sent by television.

1323
01:27:03,494 --> 01:27:05,913
Wow, what a wild trip that was.

1324
01:27:06,122 --> 01:27:08,791
It's the greatest thing
that's ever happened to me.

1325
01:27:08,875 --> 01:27:12,545
Am I coming in clear?
Mom, I said, am I coming in clear?

1326
01:27:12,628 --> 01:27:14,714
Great. He's completely unharmed.

1327
01:27:14,797 --> 01:27:16,883
You call that unharmed?

1328
01:27:16,966 --> 01:27:20,219
Wow, that was something.
Can I do it again?

1329
01:27:20,303 --> 01:27:22,054
MRS. TEEVEE: No, there'll be nothing left.

1330
01:27:22,138 --> 01:27:24,390
Don't worry about a thing, Mom.
I feel fine.

1331
01:27:24,474 --> 01:27:26,392
MIKE: I'm famous. I'm a TV star.

1332
01:27:26,476 --> 01:27:28,811
MIKE: Wait till the kids back home
hear about this.

1333
01:27:28,895 --> 01:27:30,563
MRS. TEEVEE: Nobody will.

1334
01:27:30,646 --> 01:27:33,649
Where are you taking me?
I don't want to go in there.

1335
01:27:33,733 --> 01:27:36,068
Be quiet. Well?

1336
01:27:37,069 --> 01:27:40,782
Fortunately, small boys
are extremely springy and elastic.

1337
01:27:40,907 --> 01:27:43,868
So I think we'll put him
in my special taffy-pulling machine.

1338
01:27:43,951 --> 01:27:44,994
That should do the trick.

1339
01:27:45,119 --> 01:27:46,662
To the Taffy-Pulling Room.

1340
01:27:46,746 --> 01:27:50,500
The boy is in his mother's purse.
But be extremely careful.

1341
01:27:50,583 --> 01:27:52,001
[Mrs. Teevee stuttering]

1342
01:27:52,502 --> 01:27:54,420
What's he saying?

1343
01:27:54,670 --> 01:27:57,256
No, I won't hold you responsible.

1344
01:27:58,508 --> 01:28:02,220
And now, my dearest lady,
it's time to say goodbye.

1345
01:28:02,428 --> 01:28:04,222
No, don't speak.

1346
01:28:04,430 --> 01:28:08,684
For some moments in life,
there are no words. Run along now.

1347
01:28:08,851 --> 01:28:11,562
[Mrs. Teevee mumbles incoherently]

1348
01:28:15,191 --> 01:28:17,068
<i>Adieu.</i>

1349
01:28:17,693 --> 01:28:20,363
" Parting is such sweet sorrow. "

1350
01:28:26,244 --> 01:28:28,287
[Slow instrumental music]

1351
01:28:29,372 --> 01:28:32,375
<i>[Singing] Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do</i>

1352
01:28:32,959 --> 01:28:35,795
<i>I've got another puzzle for you</i>

1353
01:28:36,504 --> 01:28:39,173
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompada Dee</i>

1354
01:28:39,966 --> 01:28:42,885
<i>If you are wise you'll listen to me</i>

1355
01:28:43,511 --> 01:28:46,514
<i>What do you get from a glut of TV?</i>

1356
01:28:46,722 --> 01:28:49,976
<i>A pain in the neck and an IQ of three</i>

1357
01:28:50,476 --> 01:28:53,896
<i>Why don't you try simply reading a book?</i>

1358
01:28:53,980 --> 01:28:56,524
<i>Or could you just not bear to look?</i>

1359
01:28:56,607 --> 01:28:58,860
<i>You'll get no</i>

1360
01:29:01,195 --> 01:29:04,115
<i>You'll get no commercials</i>

1361
01:29:04,198 --> 01:29:08,369
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompadee Da</i>

1362
01:29:08,703 --> 01:29:12,248
<i>If you like reading, you will go far</i>

1363
01:29:12,582 --> 01:29:15,835
<i>You will live in happiness, too</i>

1364
01:29:16,335 --> 01:29:17,587
<i>Like the</i>

1365
01:29:18,212 --> 01:29:19,380
<i>Oompa</i>

1366
01:29:20,214 --> 01:29:23,259
<i>Oompa Loompa Doompadee Do</i>

1367
01:29:26,929 --> 01:29:30,850
So much to do: Invoices, bills, letters.

1368
01:29:31,851 --> 01:29:34,353
I must answer that note from the Queen.

1369
01:29:35,188 --> 01:29:38,858
What's gonna happen to the other kids?
Augustus and Veruca?

1370
01:29:38,941 --> 01:29:42,195
My dear boy, I promise you
they'll be quite all right.

1371
01:29:42,278 --> 01:29:47,200
<i>When they leave here, they'll be restored
to their normal, terrible old selves.</i>

1372
01:29:47,950 --> 01:29:50,703
But maybe they'll be
a little bit wiser for the wear.

1373
01:29:50,787 --> 01:29:52,371
Don't worry about it.

1374
01:29:52,455 --> 01:29:54,874
What do we do now, Mr. Wonka?

1375
01:29:55,291 --> 01:29:59,170
I hope you enjoyed yourselves.
Excuse me for not showing you out.

1376
01:29:59,253 --> 01:30:02,673
Straight up the stairs, you'll find it.
I'm busy. A whole day wasted.

1377
01:30:02,757 --> 01:30:04,592
Goodbye to you both.

1378
01:30:05,968 --> 01:30:07,386
[Door closing]

1379
01:30:08,304 --> 01:30:11,474
What happened?
Did we do something wrong?

1380
01:30:12,058 --> 01:30:13,976
I don't know, Charlie.

1381
01:30:14,560 --> 01:30:16,646
But I'm going to find out.

1382
01:30:22,068 --> 01:30:23,861
[Clock ticking]

1383
01:30:34,288 --> 01:30:37,416
GRANDPA: Mr. Wonka?
WONKA: I am extraordinarily busy, sir.

1384
01:30:37,500 --> 01:30:40,336
I just wanted to ask about the chocolate.

1385
01:30:40,419 --> 01:30:43,840
The lifetime supply of chocolate.
For Charlie.

1386
01:30:44,340 --> 01:30:45,883
When does he get it?

1387
01:30:46,008 --> 01:30:48,136
WONKA: He doesn't.
GRANDPA: Why not?

1388
01:30:48,344 --> 01:30:50,471
WONKA: Because he broke the rules.

1389
01:30:50,680 --> 01:30:53,391
What rules?
We didn't see any rules, did we?

1390
01:30:53,474 --> 01:30:55,518
Wrong, sir!

1391
01:30:55,935 --> 01:30:59,522
Under Section 37-B
of the contract signed by him...

1392
01:30:59,605 --> 01:31:03,609
it states quite clearly that
all offers shall become null and void if...

1393
01:31:03,693 --> 01:31:07,029
And you can read it for yourself
in this photostat copy:

1394
01:31:07,113 --> 01:31:08,698
" I, the undersigned...

1395
01:31:08,781 --> 01:31:11,784
" shall forfeit all rights,
privileges, and licenses...

1396
01:31:11,868 --> 01:31:14,078
" herein contained," etc.

1397
01:31:14,287 --> 01:31:17,999
<i>'"Fax mentis incendium
gloriae culpum, '"etc.</i>

1398
01:31:21,377 --> 01:31:24,672
It's all there, black and white,
clear as crystal!

1399
01:31:24,881 --> 01:31:27,091
You stole Fizzy-Lifting drinks!

1400
01:31:27,300 --> 01:31:31,053
You bumped into the ceiling which
now has to be washed and sterilized!

1401
01:31:31,137 --> 01:31:33,181
So you get nothing!

1402
01:31:33,389 --> 01:31:34,682
You lose!

1403
01:31:34,891 --> 01:31:36,684
Good day, sir!

1404
01:31:37,810 --> 01:31:39,395
You're a crook.

1405
01:31:39,479 --> 01:31:42,482
You're a cheat and a swindler,
that's what you are.

1406
01:31:42,565 --> 01:31:44,650
How can you do a thing like this?

1407
01:31:44,734 --> 01:31:48,488
Build up a little boy's hopes and
then smash all his dreams to pieces.

1408
01:31:48,571 --> 01:31:50,615
You're an inhuman monster!

1409
01:31:50,698 --> 01:31:52,366
I said, good day!

1410
01:31:56,579 --> 01:31:59,457
Come on, Charlie. Let's get out of here.

1411
01:31:59,540 --> 01:32:02,502
I'll get even with him
if it's the last thing I ever do.

1412
01:32:02,585 --> 01:32:05,505
If Slugworth wants a Gobstopper,
he'll get one.

1413
01:32:28,486 --> 01:32:29,904
Mr. Wonka.

1414
01:32:38,371 --> 01:32:42,834
WONKA: " So shines a good deed
in a weary world. "

1415
01:32:44,585 --> 01:32:45,628
Charlie.

1416
01:32:47,421 --> 01:32:49,048
My boy.

1417
01:32:49,715 --> 01:32:51,008
<i>You've won!</i>

1418
01:32:51,217 --> 01:32:53,219
You did it!

1419
01:32:53,302 --> 01:32:56,013
I just knew you would!

1420
01:32:57,598 --> 01:33:00,601
<i>Forgive me for putting you through this.
Please forgive me.</i>

1421
01:33:00,685 --> 01:33:03,813
Come in, Mr. Wilkinson.
Charlie, meet Mr. Wilkinson.

1422
01:33:03,896 --> 01:33:05,481
WILKINSON: Pleasure.
CHARLIE: Slugworth.

1423
01:33:05,565 --> 01:33:08,234
No, that's not Slugworth.
He works for me.

1424
01:33:08,317 --> 01:33:10,736
CHARLIE: For you?
WONKA: I had to test you, Charlie.

1425
01:33:10,820 --> 01:33:13,781
And you passed the test. You won!

1426
01:33:13,865 --> 01:33:16,367
GRANDPA: Won what?
WONKA: The jackpot, my dear sir.

1427
01:33:16,451 --> 01:33:19,162
WONKA: The grand and glorious jackpot.
CHARLIE: The chocolate?

1428
01:33:19,245 --> 01:33:22,623
The chocolate, yes.
But that's just the beginning.

1429
01:33:22,707 --> 01:33:26,419
<i>We have to get on.
We have so much time and so little to do.</i>

1430
01:33:26,502 --> 01:33:28,379
WONKA: Strike that. Reverse it.

1431
01:33:28,588 --> 01:33:30,214
This way, please.

1432
01:33:30,756 --> 01:33:32,842
We'll take the Wonkavator.

1433
01:33:33,384 --> 01:33:35,887
Step in, Charlie. Grandpa Joe, sir.

1434
01:33:43,936 --> 01:33:48,024
- This is the great glass Wonkavator.
- It's an elevator.

1435
01:33:48,107 --> 01:33:50,610
It's a Wonkavator.
An elevator only goes up and down.

1436
01:33:50,693 --> 01:33:54,238
But the Wonkavator goes sideways,
slantways, longways, back ways...

1437
01:33:54,322 --> 01:33:57,700
CHARLIE: And front ways?
WONKA:... and any other ways you think of.

1438
01:33:58,034 --> 01:34:02,330
It can take you to any room by pressing
one of these buttons. Any button.

1439
01:34:02,538 --> 01:34:05,208
Press a button and zing, you're off.

1440
01:34:05,958 --> 01:34:08,628
<i>And up until now, I've pressed them all.</i>

1441
01:34:08,878 --> 01:34:10,296
Except one.

1442
01:34:11,130 --> 01:34:12,507
<i>This one.</i>

1443
01:34:13,382 --> 01:34:15,551
WONKA: Go ahead, Charlie.
CHARLIE: Me?

1444
01:34:18,888 --> 01:34:20,139
There it goes.

1445
01:34:22,308 --> 01:34:23,643
<i>Hold on tight.</i>

1446
01:34:24,143 --> 01:34:26,979
I'm not exactly sure
what's going to happen.

1447
01:34:27,730 --> 01:34:31,150
Faster. If we don't pick up speed,
we won't get through.

1448
01:34:31,234 --> 01:34:32,944
CHARLIE: Get through what?

1449
01:34:33,444 --> 01:34:35,655
- You mean, we're going...
- Up and out.

1450
01:34:35,738 --> 01:34:38,866
But the roof is glass.
It'll shatter into a thousand pieces.

1451
01:34:38,950 --> 01:34:41,369
- We'll be cut to ribbons.
- Probably.

1452
01:34:45,081 --> 01:34:46,749
<i>Hold on, everybody.</i>

1453
01:34:47,917 --> 01:34:50,253
Here it comes.

1454
01:34:54,298 --> 01:34:56,509
[Light instrumental music]

1455
01:35:05,143 --> 01:35:08,938
GRANDPA: You did it. Congratulations.
WONKA: Get up, take a look.

1456
01:35:09,021 --> 01:35:11,691
Grandpa, our town looks so pretty
from up here.

1457
01:35:11,774 --> 01:35:14,944
GRANDPA: Look over here, Charlie.
I think I see our house.

1458
01:35:16,779 --> 01:35:18,781
GRANDPA: It really looks beautiful.

1459
01:35:18,865 --> 01:35:20,825
There's my school, Grandpa.

1460
01:35:21,200 --> 01:35:23,995
[Light instrumental music continues]

1461
01:35:26,747 --> 01:35:29,292
How did you like the chocolate factory?

1462
01:35:29,375 --> 01:35:32,295
I think it's the most wonderful place
in the whole world.

1463
01:35:32,378 --> 01:35:34,547
WONKA: I'm very pleased
to hear you say that...

1464
01:35:34,630 --> 01:35:36,924
because I'm giving it to you.

1465
01:35:38,050 --> 01:35:40,470
CHARLIE: What?
WONKA: That's all right, isn't it?

1466
01:35:40,553 --> 01:35:43,389
GRANDPA: You're giving Charlie-
WONKA: I can't go on forever.

1467
01:35:43,473 --> 01:35:45,725
WONKA: And I don't really want to try.

1468
01:35:45,808 --> 01:35:48,853
So who can I trust
to run the factory when I leave...

1469
01:35:48,936 --> 01:35:50,897
and take care
of the Oompa Loompas for me?

1470
01:35:50,980 --> 01:35:52,398
Not a grownup.

1471
01:35:52,482 --> 01:35:55,902
A grownup would want to do everything
his own way, not mine.

1472
01:35:55,985 --> 01:35:59,739
That's why I decided a long time ago
that I had to find a child.

1473
01:35:59,822 --> 01:36:03,242
A very honest, loving child...

1474
01:36:03,743 --> 01:36:07,246
to whom I can tell all my most precious
candy-making secrets.

1475
01:36:07,330 --> 01:36:09,999
CHARLIE: So, you sent the Golden Tickets?
WONKA: Right.

1476
01:36:10,082 --> 01:36:12,752
<i>The factory's yours.
You can move in immediately.</i>

1477
01:36:12,835 --> 01:36:14,837
GRANDPA: And me?
WONKA: Absolutely.

1478
01:36:15,004 --> 01:36:17,590
CHARLIE: What happens to the-
WONKA: The whole family.

1479
01:36:17,673 --> 01:36:19,634
I want you to bring them all.

1480
01:36:23,179 --> 01:36:24,931
But, Charlie...

1481
01:36:25,431 --> 01:36:29,393
don't forget what happened to the man
who suddenly got everything he wanted.

1482
01:36:29,477 --> 01:36:30,853
CHARLIE: What happened?

1483
01:36:30,937 --> 01:36:33,272
He lived happily ever after.

1484
01:36:35,191 --> 01:36:37,860
[Sweeping instrumental music]

1485
01:37:30,288 --> 01:37:31,330
English - SDH

